    <node id="HISTOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Histology is the study of the anatomy of cells, tissues, and organs under a microscope, and it is also a key process used to guide clinical decision-making. In practice, histology involves the evaluation of the structure of cells or tissues at the microscopic level, typically performed by a neuropathologist on biopsy tissue. This examination is essential for classifying tumors, as the microscopic features of the tissue help determine the precise diagnosis. By revealing the cellular architecture and identifying abnormalities, histology provides critical information that is used to make treatment decisions. Whether described as a broad scientific discipline or a specific diagnostic procedure, histology serves as a foundational tool in both understanding normal biological structure and diagnosing disease, particularly in the context of neuropathology and oncology.
Relationships:
  - Source: HISTOLOGY
  - Target: NEUROPATHOLOGIST
  - Description: The neuropathologist performs histologic analysis when inspecting tissue under a microscope.
  - Source: LAB TESTING
  - Target: HISTOLOGY
  - Description: Histology is part of lab testing and is performed on biopsy tissue.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">3</data>
