For cervical cancer, EBRT is a standard treatment option that aims radiation at the cancer site and nearby lymph nodes. It is often given in small doses called fractions over a period of 5 to 6 weeks. One important side effect is that EBRT can damage the ovaries and reduce hormones needed for natural pregnancy.

For rectal cancer, external beam radiation therapy uses radiation to treat the tumor, but it can also damage the ovaries and reduce hormones needed for natural pregnancy.

For bladder cancer, EBRT is the type of radiation therapy usually used; a large machine aims radiation at the tumor area.

For breast cancer, EBRT uses a machine called a linear accelerator to aim radiation at the whole breast and lymph nodes.

Overall, external beam radiation therapy is a versatile, non-invasive cancer treatment that delivers precise radiation from outside the body to destroy or shrink tumors. It is used for a variety of cancers, with treatment schedules and side effects depending on the specific cancer type and location.
