    <node id="IMMUNOTHERAPY">
      <data key="d3">36</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Immunotherapy is a type of systemic cancer treatment that uses the body’s immune system to find and destroy cancer cells by increasing immune system activity. As a systemic therapy, it treats cancer cells throughout the body and can be used in various clinical situations. Immunotherapy may be recommended for cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) that has spread, particularly when surgery and radiation are not possible or have been unsuccessful. It is also the preferred treatment for dMMR/MSI-H cancers, often using checkpoint inhibitors such as pembrolizumab, nivolumab with or without ipilimumab. For metastatic anal cancer that does not respond to treatment or worsens, immunotherapy with an immune checkpoint inhibitor is recommended; in some anal cancer patients, it has worked well enough that surgery was no longer needed. Additionally, immunotherapy can be used before surgery to make the procedure less risky. It is also considered when cancer progresses during or after chemotherapy.

The main immunotherapy drug for primary CNS lymphoma is rituximab, a monoclonal antibody that attaches to CD20 proteins on cancer cells. Immunotherapy includes PD-1 inhibitors and other checkpoint inhibitors. Possible side effects of immunotherapy include autoimmune reactions, fatigue, fever, nausea, loss of appetite, infection, diarrhea, and constipation. Overall, immunotherapy is a versatile systemic cancer treatment that harnesses the immune system, with specific agents and indications varying by cancer type.
Relationships:
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: RITUXIMAB TREATMENT
  - Description: Rituximab is the main immunotherapy drug used for primary CNS lymphoma and is given as a treatment
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: MONOCLONAL ANTIBODY THERAPY
  - Description: Monoclonal antibody therapy is a type of immunotherapy
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: DROWSINESS
  - Description: Immunotherapy may cause drowsiness
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: CONSTIPATION
  - Description: Immunotherapy may cause constipation
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: JOINT PAIN
  - Description: Immunotherapy may cause joint pain
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: LOW BLOOD CELL COUNTS
  - Description: Immunotherapy may cause low blood cell counts
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: SERIOUS BLOOD CLOTS
  - Description: Immunotherapy may cause serious blood clots
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: RASHES
  - Description: Immunotherapy may cause rashes
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: TARGETED THERAPY
  - Description: Both are cancer treatment approaches described together; targeted therapy attacks specific cancer-cell parts, while immunotherapy uses the immune system to fight cancer.
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Description: The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) offers patient education materials that describe immunotherapy as a type of systemic therapy. In its patient guidelines, NCCN provides detailed information on immunotherapy, including its side effects and the use of checkpoint inhibitors.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Description: Chemotherapy and immunotherapy are both systemic cancer treatments, meaning they travel through the bloodstream to affect cells throughout the body. They are commonly used together or as separate options depending on the cancer type and its molecular characteristics. For instance, in certain cancers, chemotherapy is recommended for tumors that are pMMR/MSS (mismatch repair-proficient/microsatellite stable), while immunotherapy is preferred for tumors that are dMMR/MSI-H (mismatch repair-deficient/microsatellite instability-high). Both treatments are also standard systemic therapy options for bladder cancer. In the context of metastatic anal cancer, immunotherapy is specifically recommended when the disease does not respond to, or worsens after, chemotherapy. Despite their different mechanisms—chemotherapy directly targets rapidly dividing cells, while immunotherapy harnesses the immune system—the two approaches share several similar side effects, including nausea, fever, infection, diarrhea, and fatigue. Overall, chemotherapy and immunotherapy play distinct but complementary roles in cancer care, with the choice of treatment guided by tumor biology, cancer type, and response to prior therapies.
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA
  - Description: Immunotherapy treats primary CNS lymphoma
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: SUPPORTIVE CARE
  - Description: Immunotherapy is a form of treatment that can cause side effects such as fatigue, fever, nausea, and infection. Supportive care is used to help relieve these side effects associated with immunotherapy. It provides essential management to reduce discomfort and improve the patient's experience during treatment.
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Immunotherapy may be given first to shrink the tumor and make surgery less risky.
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Description: The clinical trial could involve treatment with immunotherapy.
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: SYSTEMIC THERAPY
  - Description: Immunotherapy is a type of systemic therapy.
  - Source: SYSTEMIC THERAPY
  - Target: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Description: Immunotherapy is a type of systemic therapy, meaning it is administered throughout the body rather than targeting a single localized area. As a systemic therapy, immunotherapy works by leveraging the body’s immune system to fight cancer. Specifically, it increases immune activity to help identify and destroy cancer cells. Thus, within the broader category of systemic therapy, immunotherapy is distinguished by its mechanism of enhancing the immune response to combat malignancy.
  - Source: COLECTOMY
  - Target: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Description: Checkpoint inhibitor immunotherapy is recommended before colectomy for dMMR/MSI-H cancers.
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE
  - Description: The multidisciplinary team coordinates immunotherapy as part of the treatment plan.
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: FATIGUE
  - Description: Immunotherapy may cause fatigue
  - Source: PROGRESSIVE DISEASE
  - Target: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Description: Immunotherapy may be tried for locally progressive anal cancer before proceeding with surgery
  - Source: PROGRESSION
  - Target: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Description: For patients whose cancer shows progression either during or after chemotherapy, immunotherapy is recommended as the next line of treatment for those who are eligible. This approach applies when the disease advances despite or following completion of chemotherapy, establishing immunotherapy as the standard subsequent therapeutic option.
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: LOSS OF APPETITE
  - Description: Immunotherapy may cause loss of appetite
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY AND STEM CELL RESCUE
  - Description: Immunotherapy drugs such as rituximab are often given together with chemotherapy as part of cancer treatment.
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: CHECKPOINT INHIBITORS
  - Description: Checkpoint inhibitors are one type of immunotherapy used for bladder cancer.
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: PD-1 INHIBITOR
  - Description: PD-1 inhibitors are a type of immunotherapy used to treat squamous cell skin cancer and other cancers.</data>
