ENTITY: MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY (MSI)
Description: A condition in which cancer cells have more than the normal number of microsatellites due to unrepaired DNA errors; when high it is called MSI-H.
Relationships:
  - Source: MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY (MSI)
  - Target: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
  - Description: MSI-H is high microsatellite instability, often due to dMMR genes, and is part of the MSI-H/dMMR tumor mutation status.
  - Source: MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY (MSI)
  - Target: DEFICIENT MISMATCH REPAIR (DMMR)
  - Description: Deficient mismatch repair prevents DNA errors from being fixed, leading to microsatellite instability.

ENTITY: HOMOLOGOUS RECOMBINATION DEFICIENCY
Description: A DNA repair deficiency, often called HRD-positive, that can make PARP inhibitor maintenance therapy an option.
Relationships:
  - Source: HOMOLOGOUS RECOMBINATION DEFICIENCY
  - Target: BIOMARKER TESTING
  - Description: HRD status is one of the biomarkers tested to guide treatment.

ENTITY: IMMUNOTHERAPIES
Description: Treatments for which biomarker testing may determine eligibility
Relationships:
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPIES
  - Target: (none listed in data)
  - Description: (none listed in data)

ENTITY: TUMOR TESTING
Description: Also called somatic testing, molecular tumor testing, or tumor profiling; analyzes tumor tissue, metastatic biopsies, or blood containing tumor DNA to identify molecular markers and mutations in DNA repair genes
Relationships:
  - Source: TUMOR TESTING
  - Target: MUTATION
  - Description: Tumor testing looks for specific abnormalities and mutations in DNA repair genes
  - Source: TUMOR TESTING
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: A biopsy of a tumor or metastasis provides the tissue sample used for tumor testing
  - Source: TUMOR TESTING
  - Target: METASTASIS
  - Description: Tumor testing can use a sample from a metastasis in lymph nodes, bones, liver, lung, or another affected area
  - Source: TUMOR TESTING
  - Target: TUMOR STAGING
  - Description: Tumor testing helps determine the likelihood of cancer spread and aggressive transformation, which can inform tumor staging
  - Source: SOMATIC MUTATION
  - Target: TUMOR TESTING
  - Description: Tumor testing detects somatic mutations or abnormalities in tumor DNA and DNA repair genes

ENTITY: LYNCH SYNDROME
Description: Lynch syndrome is an inherited condition caused by mutations in mismatch repair (MMR) genes. It is a hereditary disorder that significantly increases the risk of developing several types of cancer, most notably colon cancer, as well as uterine (endometrial), ovarian, and other cancers. Lynch syndrome can also cause hereditary ovarian cancer and may increase the risk of developing an adrenal tumor. Because of its strong hereditary nature and elevated cancer risks, Lynch syndrome is recognized as a factor that may make germline genetic testing appropriate for affected individuals and their families.
Relationships:
  - Source: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Target: (none listed in data)
  - Description: (none listed in data)

ENTITY: TUMOR MUTATION TESTING
Description: TUMOR MUTATION TESTING is a diagnostic approach that analyzes a sample of tumor tissue or blood to detect DNA mutations present in cancer cells. This testing enables the identification of specific genetic alterations that may guide targeted therapy, allowing treatment to be tailored to the molecular characteristics of a patient’s tumor. A key function of tumor mutation testing is its ability to distinguish somatic mutations—those acquired in cancer cells—from inherited (germline) mutations, which is important for determining whether a mutation is tumor-specific or hereditary. This testing is closely related to biomarker testing and molecular profiling, as it looks for mutations in tumor DNA to inform diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment decisions. In summary, tumor mutation testing is a precision medicine tool that uses genetic information from tumors to support personalized cancer care.
Relationships:
  - Source: TUMOR MUTATION TESTING
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: A tumor or blood sample from a biopsy is used for tumor mutation testing.
  - Source: MUTATION
  - Target: TUMOR MUTATION TESTING
  - Description: Tumor mutation testing detects DNA mutations in tumor samples.
  - Source: CANCER
  - Target: TUMOR MUTATION TESTING
  - Description: Tumor mutation testing examines cancer cells for DNA mutations that may be targeted with specific therapies.

ENTITY: IMMUNOTHERAPY
Description: Immunotherapy is a type of systemic cancer treatment that uses the body’s immune system to find and destroy cancer cells by increasing immune system activity. As a systemic therapy, it treats cancer cells throughout the body and can be used in various clinical situations. Immunotherapy may be recommended for cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) that has spread, particularly when surgery and radiation are not possible or have been unsuccessful. It is also the preferred treatment for dMMR/MSI-H cancers, often using checkpoint inhibitors such as pembrolizumab, nivolumab with or without ipilimumab. For metastatic anal cancer that does not respond to treatment or worsens, immunotherapy with an immune checkpoint inhibitor is recommended; in some anal cancer patients, it has worked well enough that surgery was no longer needed. Additionally, immunotherapy can be used before surgery to make the procedure less risky. It is also considered when cancer progresses during or after chemotherapy.

The main immunotherapy drug for primary CNS lymphoma is rituximab, a monoclonal antibody that attaches to CD20 proteins on cancer cells. Immunotherapy includes PD-1 inhibitors and other checkpoint inhibitors. Possible side effects of immunotherapy include autoimmune reactions, fatigue, fever, nausea, loss of appetite, infection, diarrhea, and constipation. Overall, immunotherapy is a versatile systemic cancer treatment that harnesses the immune system, with specific agents and indications varying by cancer type.
Relationships:
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: (none listed in data)
  - Description: (none listed in data)

ENTITY: TUMOR MUTATIONAL BURDEN (TMB)
Description: The number of mutations per million base pairs of tumor DNA; called TMB-H when there are 10 or more mutations per million base pairs.
Relationships:
  - Source: TUMOR MUTATIONAL BURDEN (TMB)
  - Target: (none listed in data)
