    <node id="NICOTINE WITHDRAWAL">
      <data key="d3">5</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Nicotine withdrawal is the challenging condition experienced by most people who smoke or vape when they attempt to quit. It is a process that arises as the body and brain react to the absence of nicotine, making quitting difficult. For individuals who smoke or vape, withdrawal represents a significant hurdle in their efforts to stop using tobacco or nicotine products. The experience is characterized by a range of physical and psychological symptoms that can vary in intensity, but the overarching effect is that quitting becomes a demanding endeavor. The information consistently describes nicotine withdrawal as a difficult, challenging process that most smokers and vapers face when trying to quit, emphasizing that it is a natural but tough part of cessation. In summary, nicotine withdrawal is the difficult physiological and psychological response to nicotine cessation, which most people who smoke or vape must navigate when attempting to quit, and it is a primary reason why quitting is so hard.
Relationships:
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: NICOTINE WITHDRAWAL
  - Description: Nicotine withdrawal is challenging for most people who smoke.
  - Source: VAPING
  - Target: NICOTINE WITHDRAWAL
  - Description: Nicotine withdrawal is challenging for most people who vape.
  - Source: SMOKING OR VAPING
  - Target: NICOTINE WITHDRAWAL
  - Description: Nicotine withdrawal is challenging for most people who smoke or vape
  - Source: QUITTING SMOKING
  - Target: NICOTINE WITHDRAWAL
  - Description: Nicotine withdrawal is challenging for people who smoke and try to quit
  - Source: PATIENT
  - Target: NICOTINE WITHDRAWAL
  - Description: The stress of having cancer may make it harder for the patient to quit and manage nicotine withdrawal</data>
