ENTITY: CLINICAL STAGING
Description: Clinical staging is the rating of cancer assigned before any treatment is initiated. It is determined based on a physical exam, biopsy, and imaging tests, as well as other diagnostic evaluations. This staging system provides a baseline assessment of the extent of the disease prior to therapy. Examples of clinical staging designations include cN1, cN2, and cM0, where the prefix "c" indicates that the staging is clinical, based on initial examination and tests rather than surgical findings. The clinical stage helps guide treatment decisions and serves as a reference point for evaluating the effectiveness of subsequent interventions.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

ENTITY: IMAGING
Description: IMAGING refers to the use of diagnostic tests and scans to produce detailed pictures of the inside of the body for cancer evaluation. It includes a wide range of modalities such as X-rays, computed tomography (CT), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), positron emission tomography (PET), PET-CT, ultrasound (including transvaginal and endorectal), mammography, and bone scans. Imaging is interpreted by a radiologist and helps reveal a tumor’s location, size, and characteristics, determine whether cancer has spread beyond the primary site, guide treatment decisions, and monitor for recurrence or response to therapy.

In initial cancer staging, imaging is used to determine the extent of disease before treatment. For cervical cancer, initial imaging may include CT, MRI, PET, and/or transvaginal ultrasound. For prostate cancer, imaging tests are used alongside PSA tests and digital rectal exams to provide a more complete picture of the prostate and help decide whether a biopsy is needed. Imaging also helps determine whether prostate cancer has spread. For colon cancer, CT with contrast is the main test used to assess the extent of disease in the body. In primary CNS lymphoma, MRI or CT scans are used to detect tumors and aid diagnosis. In some cases, imaging is also used to evaluate basal cell skin cancer.

After treatment, imaging plays a key role in evaluating symptoms or suspected recurrence. For cervical cancer, post-treatment imaging may include CT, MRI, and/or PET/CT scans, typically from the neck to the midthigh, if recurrence or spread is suspected. However, routine radiologic imaging is generally not needed at regular intervals after cervical cancer treatment unless there is a clinical suspicion of recurrence. In contrast, for stage II and III anal cancers in complete remission, radiologic imaging such as CT or MRI may be recommended every year for three years to check for spread. For rectal cancer after endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD) or transanal local excision, surveillance imaging with endorectal ultrasound or pelvic MRI with contrast is recommended to check for recurrence. After treatment for recurrence, yearly imaging using CT and/or MRI may be performed.

Imaging results are often kept in a medical binder, and patients are advised to obtain copies of their imaging results for their records. Although the specific imaging modality and schedule vary by cancer type and clinical context, the overall purpose of imaging is to provide essential anatomical and functional information that guides cancer diagnosis, staging, and management.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

ENTITY: TUMOR STAGING
Description: TUMOR STAGING is the process of determining how advanced cancer is and whether it has spread to other parts of the body. It involves combining T, N, and M scores derived from imaging scans, biopsies, and blood tests to describe the extent of cancer in the body, including the size and spread of the tumor. This comprehensive assessment helps characterize the overall cancer burden and progression.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

ENTITY: IMAGING TESTS
Description: Imaging tests are diagnostic procedures that create pictures of the inside of the body to evaluate cancer. They are used across many cancer types to diagnose, stage, and guide treatment. The tests can reveal the presence, size, location, and shape of a primary tumor, and determine whether cancer has spread to nearby tissues, organs, lymph nodes, or distant parts of the body. This information is essential for clinical staging, assessing tumor spread, and planning treatment.

Common imaging modalities include X-rays, computed tomography (CT), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), positron emission tomography (PET), FDG-PET/CT, ultrasound, mammograms, and bone scans. Some tests, such as transrectal ultrasound or MRI, are used specifically to see if cancer has spread beyond the prostate, and may not be needed for early-stage prostate cancer. Mammograms create pictures of the breast and are used in evaluating breast cancer and determining treatment planning. Ultrasound can be used to diagnose and confirm liver cancer. CT, MRI, and FDG-PET/CT are used to image tumors of the head and neck, such as nasopharyngeal carcinoma, helping determine stage and treatment plan. Imaging tests also play a role in ovarian cancer by showing tumor location, size, and shape, and whether it has spread beyond the ovaries before surgery. For bladder cancer, scans show whether the cancer has spread to nearby tissues, organs, lymph nodes, or distant parts of the body. For esophageal cancer, imaging tests are used to diagnose and determine the extent of the disease. In leukemia, imaging tests such as CT, MRI, and PET scans are used to look for leukemia outside the bone marrow, as well as areas of infection or bleeding.

Imaging tests are used during the initial workup and also during follow-up and surveillance to evaluate whether cancer has returned. A radiologist typically interprets the images and sends a report to the patient's doctor. Some imaging tests require contrast material and specific preparation. Overall, these tests are indispensable in cancer care for detecting cancer deposits, determining the extent of disease, and informing treatment decisions. By taking pictures of the inside of the body, imaging tests help the care team stage cancer, plan treatment, and monitor patients over time. They are performed in a variety of clinical settings, from initial diagnosis through long-term follow-up, and are adapted to the specific cancer type and clinical question. Whether assessing a primary tumor, checking for metastasis, or confirming a diagnosis, imaging tests provide critical visual information that guides medical decision-making. Their results are used in treatment planning for many cancers, including breast, nasopharyngeal, ovarian, bladder, esophageal, liver, and prostate cancers. In summary, imaging tests are a cornerstone of modern oncology, enabling precise evaluation of cancer and its spread.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

ENTITY: MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING (MRI)
Description: Imaging test used to examine anal cancer extent and nearby lymph nodes
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

ENTITY: LYMPH NODE EVALUATION
Description: Assessment of pelvic or para-aortic lymph nodes during surgery or staging to determine whether cervical cancer has spread
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

ENTITY: BREAST CANCER STAGING
Description: Breast cancer staging is the application of cancer staging to breast cancer, used to reflect prognosis and guide treatment decisions; it is often done before and after surgery.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

ENTITY: RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY WITH PELVIC LYMPH NODE EVALUATION
Description: A surgical procedure recommended for stage 1A2 and 1B1 cancers that do not meet low-risk criteria and for stage 1B2 and 2A1 cancers; para-aortic lymph nodes may also be removed
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

ENTITY: CT
Description: CT (computed tomography) is an imaging test used in the diagnosis and management of cervical cancer. As part of initial imaging, CT helps determine the extent of the disease before treatment begins, providing critical information for staging and treatment planning. It is also used for diagnosis, and the contrast material used for CT can differ from that used in MRI. CT can be combined with positron emission tomography (PET) as a PET/CT scan, and it can be used to guide a fine-needle biopsy. In follow-up care, CT with contrast is an imaging option recommended for patients with stage 2, 3, and 4A cervical cancer and for checking for possible recurrence of the disease.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

ENTITY: LABORATORY TESTING
Description: Laboratory testing is the examination of surgically removed tissue and lymph nodes to detect cancer and guide staging.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

ENTITY: COMPUTED TOMOGRAPHY (CT)
Description: Imaging test using x-rays to create detailed pictures; used to assess extent and spread of anal cancer in the anus, pelvis, chest, and abdomen
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.
</data>
