      <data key="d4">ENTITY: GERMLINE MUTATION
Description: A germline mutation is a gene mutation inherited from a birth parent. It is an inherited mutation present in every cell of the body and is passed down in families, which is why it is also called a hereditary mutation. Germline mutations can increase an individual's cancer risk, can be passed on to children, and may also be present in blood relatives.
Relationships:
  - Source: GERMLINE MUTATION
  - Target: HEREDITARY GENE TESTING
  - Description: Hereditary gene testing looks for inherited germline mutations in every cell</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TUMOR MUTATION TESTING">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">TUMOR MUTATION TESTING is a diagnostic approach that analyzes a sample of tumor tissue or blood to detect DNA mutations present in cancer cells. This testing enables the identification of specific genetic alterations that may guide targeted therapy, allowing treatment to be tailored to the molecular characteristics of a patient’s tumor. A key function of tumor mutation testing is its ability to distinguish somatic mutations—those acquired in cancer cells—from inherited (germline) mutations, which is important for determining whether a mutation is tumor-specific or hereditary. This testing is closely related to biomarker testing and molecular profiling, as it looks for mutations in tumor DNA to inform diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment decisions. In summary, tumor mutation testing is a precision medicine tool that uses genetic information from tumors to support personalized cancer care.
Relationships:
  - Source: TUMOR MUTATION TESTING
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: A tumor or blood sample from a biopsy is used for tumor mutation testing.
  - Source: MUTATION
  - Target: TUMOR MUTATION TESTING
  - Description: Tumor mutation testing detects DNA mutations in tumor samples.
  - Source: CANCER
  - Target: TUMOR MUTATION TESTING
  - Description: Tumor mutation testing examines cancer cells for DNA mutations that may be targeted with specific therapies.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">4</data>
