    <node id="LEUKEMIA">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">LEUKEMIA is a cancer of blood-forming tissues, characterized by the abnormal proliferation of blood cells. This disease can spread beyond the bloodstream to other parts of the body, including the lymph nodes, liver, spleen, and skin, and, in rare cases, it may affect the lining of the brain and spinal cord. Additionally, leukemia can travel to the cerebrospinal fluid surrounding the spine or brain, where it may cause symptoms such as headaches, neck pain, and sensitivity to light. The development of leukemia can be influenced by inherited leukemia predisposition syndromes, which contribute to an individual's genetic risk for the disease.
Relationships:
  - Source: LEUKEMIA
  - Target: LEUKEMIA PREDISPOSITION SYNDROMES
  - Description: Leukemia predisposition syndromes are inherited conditions that increase the risk of leukemia</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">2</data>
