    <node id="X-RAY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">X-ray is a form of medical imaging that uses small amounts of radiation to create pictures of the inside of the body, also known as a radiograph. As an imaging technique, it relies on radiation to visualize internal structures and is commonly employed in a variety of examinations, including CT scans, chest imaging, and pyelograms. A CT scan is described as a more detailed kind of x-ray, providing greater cross-sectional detail than a standard x-ray. A chest x-ray is a specific x-ray examination focused on the chest area. While x-rays use radiation, other imaging modalities such as MRI do not use radiation. Overall, x-ray is a foundational imaging method that uses controlled, low-dose radiation for diagnostic purposes, with CT as a more advanced application and chest x-ray as a targeted exam.
**Relationships:** (No explicit relationships listed in the provided data for this entity)

---</data>
