    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: CERVICAL CANCER → IMAGING">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Cervical cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the cervix. Imaging plays a critical role in the clinical management of cervical cancer, particularly in the pre-treatment phase. Before any therapeutic intervention is initiated, imaging is used to determine the extent of the cancer—a process known as staging. By evaluating how far the cancer has spread within the cervix, into nearby tissues, or to distant organs, imaging provides essential information that guides treatment planning. This pre-treatment assessment helps clinicians choose the most appropriate therapeutic approach, whether surgery, radiation, chemotherapy, or a combination of these modalities. Accurate staging via imaging is therefore fundamental to optimizing outcomes for patients diagnosed with cervical cancer.

---</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
