    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: IMAGING → MRI">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) is a type of IMAGING test used to create detailed pictures of the inside of the body. As an imaging modality, MRI serves as an initial diagnostic tool and is frequently employed in oncology, particularly for cervical cancer. Specifically, MRI is one of the initial imaging tests used for cervical cancer staging, helping clinicians assess the extent of disease at diagnosis. It is also one of the imaging tests used in surveillance after recurrence treatment, allowing for monitoring of patients following therapy for recurrent disease. Additionally, MRI with contrast is utilized as an imaging method for pelvic follow-up of cervical cancer, with particular importance after fertility-sparing treatment. Thus, MRI functions across multiple stages of cervical cancer care: initial evaluation/staging, post-treatment surveillance, and long-term pelvic follow-up, depending on the clinical context and whether contrast enhancement is used. As a versatile IMAGING technique, MRI provides essential anatomical and pathological information that guides diagnosis, treatment planning, and monitoring in cervical cancer management.

---</data>
