    <node id="THROAT CANCER">
      <data key="d3">8</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Throat cancer is a general term used to describe a group of cancers that affect the throat and nearby structures. It encompasses several distinct types, including oropharyngeal cancer, laryngeal cancer, hypopharyngeal cancer, esophageal cancer, and nasopharyngeal cancer. As a category, throat cancer is classified as a head and neck cancer. One notable subtype, oropharyngeal cancer, is most often related to human papillomavirus (HPV) infection. Throat cancer is also the subject of the NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Throat Cancer, which provide patient-focused information on diagnosis, treatment, and supportive care. Overall, throat cancer represents a diverse set of malignancies with varying risk factors, presentations, and treatment approaches, unified by their location in the throat region.
Relationships:
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: THROAT CANCER
  - Description: Nasopharyngeal cancer affects the nasopharynx, an upper part of the throat, so it is a type of throat cancer</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d4">ENTITY: THROAT CANCER
Description: Throat cancer is a general term used to describe a group of cancers that affect the throat and nearby structures. It encompasses several distinct types, including oropharyngeal cancer, laryngeal cancer, hypopharyngeal cancer, esophageal cancer, and nasopharyngeal cancer. As a category, throat cancer is classified as a head and neck cancer. One notable subtype, oropharyngeal cancer, is most often related to human papillomavirus (HPV) infection. Throat cancer is also the subject of the NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Throat Cancer, which provide patient-focused information on diagnosis, treatment, and supportive care. Overall, throat cancer represents a diverse set of malignancies with varying risk factors, presentations, and treatment approaches, unified by their location in the throat region.
Relationships:
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: THROAT CANCER
  - Description: Nasopharyngeal cancer affects the nasopharynx, an upper part of the throat, so it is a type of throat cancer</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOUTH CANCER">
      <data key="d3">2</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Mouth cancer, also known as oral cancer or oral cavity cancer, is a malignant condition that develops in the oral cavity and is classified as a type of head and neck cancer. It can arise in various parts of the mouth, including the lips, gums (gingiva), buccal mucosa (the inner lining of the cheeks), the front two-thirds of the tongue, the hard palate, the alveolar ridge, the floor of the mouth, and the retromolar trigone (the area behind the wisdom teeth). The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides detailed information and clinical guidance on this disease. Mouth cancer is characterized by the uncontrolled growth of cells in these oral structures, and its management typically involves a multidisciplinary approach.
Relationships:
  - (No direct relationships listed in the provided data for this entity)</data>
