    <node id="HEPATITIS">
      <data key="d3">6</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Hepatitis is a liver condition and a type of liver disease, primarily caused by viral infections such as hepatitis B and hepatitis C. It is recognized as a significant risk factor for liver cancer. The disease can progress through several severe stages, including chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis, liver failure, and ultimately liver cancer. These complications highlight the serious long-term consequences of hepatitis, particularly when the underlying viral infection is not managed effectively. As a major public health concern, hepatitis affects liver function and can lead to life-threatening outcomes if left untreated.
Relationships:
  - Source: HEPATITIS
  - Target: HEPATITIS B
  - Description: Hepatitis B is one of the viruses that cause hepatitis.
  - Source: HEPATITIS
  - Target: HEPATITIS C
  - Description: Hepatitis C is one of the viruses that cause hepatitis.
  - Source: HEPATITIS
  - Target: LIVER CANCER
  - Description: Hepatitis is a recognized risk factor for the development of liver cancer. The condition can lead to liver cancer over time, making it an important contributing factor in the disease’s progression.
  - Source: CIRRHOSIS
  - Target: HEPATITIS
  - Description: Hepatitis can cause cirrhosis.
  - Source: HEPATITIS
  - Target: LIVER FAILURE
  - Description: Hepatitis can cause liver failure.
  - Source: HEPATITIS
  - Target: LIVER DISEASE
  - Description: Hepatitis is a type of liver disease.</data>
