    <node id="ONCOLOGY NURSE">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">ONCOLOGY NURSE is a specialized nursing professional who works in cancer care. Oncology nurses provide hands-on care to patients, including administering systemic therapies such as chemotherapy, managing overall patient care, answering questions, and helping patients cope with treatment side effects. They are sometimes referred to as nurse navigators, guiding patients through the complexities of cancer treatment. Additionally, oncology nurses may offer counseling to patients who are at genetic risk of developing skin cancer, supporting prevention and early detection efforts. This role combines clinical expertise, patient education, and emotional support across the cancer care continuum.
Relationships:
  - Source: ONCOLOGY NURSE
  - Target: NURSE NAVIGATOR
  - Description: Oncology nurses are sometimes called nurse navigators
  - Source: ONCOLOGY NURSE
  - Target: CARE TEAM
  - Description: The care team may include oncology nurses
  - Source: ONCOLOGY NURSE
  - Target: SYSTEMIC THERAPY
  - Description: Oncology nurses provide hands-on care, including giving systemic therapy
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: ONCOLOGY NURSE
  - Description: NCCN recommends that patients at genetic risk of skin cancer receive counseling from specialists such as oncology nurses</data>
