    <node id="LYMPHOMA">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Lymphoma is a cancer of lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell that plays a central role in the immune system. It is characterized by an abnormal overgrowth of these cells, which can accumulate in lymph nodes and other tissues. One specific form is primary CNS lymphoma, which arises in the central nervous system. In the context of treatment described here, high-dose chemotherapy is administered to kill lymphoma cells throughout the body during a procedure known as a stem cell rescue, which helps restore healthy blood-forming cells after the intensive therapy.
Relationships:
  - Source: LYMPHOMA
  - Target: PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA
  - Description: Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of lymphoma
  - Source: LYMPHOMA
  - Target: CANCER
  - Description: Lymphoma is a cancer of lymphocytes
  - Source: LYMPHOMA
  - Target: OVERGROWTH
  - Description: Lymphoma is an abnormal overgrowth of lymphocytes
  - Source: STEM CELL RESCUE
  - Target: LYMPHOMA
  - Description: Stem cell rescue is used when lymphoma comes back after earlier treatment or after other treatments
  - Source: HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: LYMPHOMA
  - Description: High-dose chemotherapy is given to kill lymphoma cells in the body</data>
