    <node id="LYMPH NODE BIOPSY">
      <data key="d3">1</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Lymph node biopsy is a diagnostic procedure in which a sample of tissue is removed from a lymph node for laboratory testing. It is commonly performed when cancer spread to the lymph nodes is suspected, allowing pathologists to determine whether malignant cells are present. The procedure may be guided by imaging techniques such as ultrasound or CT scan to ensure accurate targeting of the affected node. Additionally, lymph node biopsy may be utilized when the BPDCN subtype of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is suspected, as it provides essential tissue for confirming the diagnosis. Overall, this biopsy is a key tool for detecting cancer involvement and supporting diagnosis in specific hematologic conditions.
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d4">ENTITY: LYMPH NODE BIOPSY
Description: Lymph node biopsy is a diagnostic procedure in which a sample of tissue is removed from a lymph node for laboratory testing. It is commonly performed when cancer spread to the lymph nodes is suspected, allowing pathologists to determine whether malignant cells are present. The procedure may be guided by imaging techniques such as ultrasound or CT scan to ensure accurate targeting of the affected node. Additionally, lymph node biopsy may be utilized when the BPDCN subtype of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is suspected, as it provides essential tissue for confirming the diagnosis. Overall, this biopsy is a key tool for detecting cancer involvement and supporting diagnosis in specific hematologic conditions.
