    <node id="VAPING">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Vaping is the use of e-cigarettes. Vaping nicotine increases cancer risk, can limit how well cancer treatment works, and increases surgical side effects. For these reasons, the advice is to quit vaping.
Relationships:
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: VAPING
  - Description: Smoking and vaping are both routes of nicotine use and are discussed together in the text. The text groups smoking and vaping together as behaviors to quit because they can affect cancer treatment.
  - Source: VAPING
  - Target: QUITTING SMOKING
  - Description: The text advises people who vape to seek help to quit
  - Source: SMOKEFREE.GOV
  - Target: VAPING
  - Description: SmokeFree.gov is also recommended as support for people who vape
  - Source: BETOBACCOFREE.GOV
  - Target: VAPING
  - Description: BeTobaccoFree.gov is also recommended as support for people who vape
  - Source: VAPING
  - Target: NICOTINE WITHDRAWAL
  - Description: Nicotine withdrawal is challenging for most people who vape.
  - Source: VAPING
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Vaping can limit how well cancer treatment works
  - Source: CARE TEAM
  - Target: VAPING
  - Description: The care team can help a patient quit vaping through counseling and medicines
  - Source: CDC.GOV/TOBACCO
  - Target: VAPING
  - Description: CDC.gov/tobacco is also recommended as support for people who vape
  - Source: SURGERY
  - Target: VAPING
  - Description: Vaping greatly increases the chance of side effects during and after surgery.</data>
