  - Source: NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
  - Target: CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE
  - Description: The National Cancer Institute (NCI) operates the Cancer Information Service (CIS), which is an integral part of the Institute. The CIS functions as NCI's dedicated service for providing cancer information and clinical trial assistance. It helps both patients and the general public find relevant clinical trials and offers detailed information about clinical trial options. As a core component of the National Cancer Institute, the Cancer Information Service serves as a reliable resource for those seeking guidance on cancer-related topics and access to clinical trial information.
  - Source: NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
  - Target: NCI'S CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE
  - Description: The Cancer Information Service is part of the National Cancer Institute and helps people find clinical trials.
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
  - Description: The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) and the National Cancer Institute (NCI) are both prominent cancer-focused organizations that serve as authoritative resources for cancer information, clinical trials, and treatment guidance. Both entities are consistently identified in the source material as key resources for patients and providers seeking to find clinical trials and obtain guidance on cancer treatment. NCCN is recognized for providing patient guidelines as well as information about cancer centers, offering practical, structured guidance to help patients understand their treatment options. NCI is recognized for providing clinical trial search capabilities, enabling patients, caregivers, and researchers to identify relevant ongoing studies. Together, they offer complementary resources within the cancer information landscape: NCCN contributes trusted patient-facing guidelines and a network of cancer centers, while NCI contributes a robust clinical trial search tool. Both are listed as cancer-focused organizations that patients can consult when seeking information about clinical trials and cancer treatment guidance.
  - Source: NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
  - Target: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Description: The National Cancer Institute (NCI) provides comprehensive clinical trial information and search resources. These resources are available through the NCI's official website at cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/clinical-trials/search, where patients, caregivers, and researchers can locate ongoing clinical trials. In addition to the online search tool, the National Cancer Institute's Cancer Information Service is also available to help individuals find clinical trials that may be relevant to their needs. Together, these offerings ensure that the public has access to both self-service search tools and personalized assistance when seeking information about clinical trials.
  - Source: NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
  - Target: UNITED STATES
  - Description: The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a U.S. government organization operating within the United States. It is formally a U.S. government agency and institute, and it is located in the United States. The NCI is also listed under "In the United States" as a resource for finding clinical trials, reflecting its role in providing access to cancer research and treatment information. As a government entity, the NCI operates as part of the U.S. federal structure, dedicated to cancer research and related public health efforts.
  - Source: NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
  - Target: CLINICAL TRIALS
  - Description: The National Cancer Institute (NCI) serves as a key resource for patients and others seeking information about clinical trials. As part of its mission, NCI provides a dedicated clinical-trials search tool on its official website, cancer.gov, enabling users to find relevant studies. In addition to this search functionality, NCI actively supports patients throughout the process of identifying and accessing clinical trials, reinforcing its role as a trusted resource in cancer research and care.
  - Source: NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
  - Target: U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE
  - Description: The National Cancer Institute (NCI) and the U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) are both U.S. government health organizations and official resources for finding clinical trials. NCI provides cancer-specific clinical trial search tools, including access through cancer.gov, while NLM maintains ClinicalTrials.gov, a comprehensive database of clinical studies. Together, these two entities serve as key sources for patients, researchers, and the public seeking information on clinical trials, with NCI focusing on cancer-related trials and NLM offering a broader clinical trial registry. Both are listed as reliable, government-supported resources for clinical-trial searching.
  - Source: NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
  - Target: U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION
  - Description: The FDA and NCI are U.S. government agencies involved in cancer treatment and clinical research; the FDA approves treatments while NCI provides trial resources.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
  - Description: NCI provides a portal for patients to search for cancer clinical trials.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIALS
  - Target: NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
  - Description: NCI provides clinical trial search information at cancer.gov and supports the Cancer Information Service.
  - Source: U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION
  - Target: NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
  - Description: Both are U.S. organizations involved in the clinical trial process: NCI helps patients find trials, and FDA approves treatments found safe and effective in trials.
  - Source: FDA
  - Target: NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
  - Description: NCI provides information on clinical trials that may eventually lead to FDA-approved treatments.
  - Source: NCCN CANCER CENTERS
  - Target: NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
  - Description: The National Cancer Institute (NCI) and NCCN Cancer Centers are both U.S. organizations listed as resources for finding cancer clinical trials. They serve as authoritative sources for locating clinical trials available in the United States. NCCN Cancer Centers can be accessed online at NCCN.org/cancercenters, while the NCI's corresponding resource is available at cancer.gov. Both are recognized for providing information on cancer clinical trials to patients, caregivers, and researchers.</data>
    </node>
