    <node id="PRIMARY LIVER CANCER">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Primary liver cancer is cancer that originates in the liver. The most common type is hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC). In adults, other types of primary liver cancer include intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, sarcomas, and mixed-type tumors.
Relationships:
  - Source: PRIMARY LIVER CANCER
  - Target: LIVER CANCER
  - Description: Primary liver cancer is cancer that starts in the liver, meaning it is a specific category of liver cancer. It is distinct from cancers that spread to the liver from other parts of the body. As a type of liver cancer, primary liver cancer originates within the liver itself, and it falls under the broader classification of liver cancer.
  - Source: PRIMARY LIVER CANCER
  - Target: HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA
  - Description: Hepatocellular carcinoma is the most common type of primary liver cancer in adults
  - Source: PRIMARY LIVER CANCER
  - Target: INTRAHEPATIC CHOLANGIOCARCINOMA
  - Description: Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma is the second most common type of primary liver cancer in adults
  - Source: PRIMARY LIVER CANCER
  - Target: SARCOMA
  - Description: Rare sarcomas that start in blood vessel cells are a type of primary liver cancer
  - Source: PRIMARY LIVER CANCER
  - Target: MIXED-TYPE TUMOR
  - Description: A mixed-type tumor is a rare type of primary liver cancer containing both hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma
  - Source: PRIMARY LIVER CANCER
  - Target: HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA (HCC)
  - Description: HCC is the most common type of primary liver cancer.
  - Source: ALPHA-FETOPROTEIN (AFP) TEST
  - Target: PRIMARY LIVER CANCER
  - Description: Elevated or rising AFP may be a sign of primary liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
