  - Description: FDG-PET/CT and sodium fluoride PET/CT are two imaging techniques used to evaluate cancer, particularly in bone. When FDG-PET/CT clearly demonstrates the presence of cancer in the bone, a subsequent sodium fluoride PET/CT scan may not be required. In other words, if the FDG-PET/CT already provides unambiguous evidence of bone involvement, the additional information from a sodium fluoride PET/CT is considered unnecessary. This guidance applies to both modalities, emphasizing that a clear positive result on FDG-PET/CT can eliminate the need for further sodium fluoride PET/CT imaging in the same clinical context.</data>
