    <node id="PREMATURE MENOPAUSE">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Premature menopause is a condition in which menopause occurs before its natural time, and it can be triggered by medical interventions. One cause is radiation therapy, specifically when the ovaries are included in the radiation field; this results in early menopause with symptoms similar to those of surgical menopause. Another cause is surgical removal of both ovaries before natural menopause, known as surgical menopause, which is characterized by symptoms such as hot flashes, sleep problems, and mood changes. Together, these descriptions indicate that premature menopause may arise from cancer treatments or prophylactic surgeries, and its clinical presentation commonly includes vasomotor symptoms like hot flashes, along with sleep disturbances and mood alterations.
Relationships:
  - Source: PREMATURE MENOPAUSE
  - Target: SURGICAL MENOPAUSE
  - Description: Premature menopause has symptoms similar to surgical menopause.
  - Source: PREMATURE MENOPAUSE
  - Target: RADIATION-INDUCED MENOPAUSE
  - Description: Radiation-induced menopause is premature menopause caused by radiation therapy.
  - Source: MENOPAUSE
  - Target: PREMATURE MENOPAUSE
  - Description: Premature menopause is early menopause.
  - Source: PREMATURE MENOPAUSE
  - Target: HYSTERECTOMY AND BSO
  - Description: Surgery that removes both ovaries causes premature menopause if the patient has not already entered menopause
  - Source: PREMATURE MENOPAUSE
  - Target: OVARIES
  - Description: Inclusion of the ovaries in radiation can cause premature menopause.
  - Source: PREMATURE MENOPAUSE
  - Target: HORMONE REPLACEMENT THERAPY
  - Description: Hormone replacement therapy may be suggested to manage symptoms of premature/surgical menopause</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">6</data>
