    <node id="HYSTERECTOMY">
      <data key="d3">12</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the uterus, which permanently eliminates the possibility of carrying a pregnancy. In the context of cervical cancer, this surgery typically includes removal of the cervix as well as the uterus, making it a treatment option for the disease. However, hysterectomy is only considered an appropriate treatment for cervical cancer when the cancer has not spread beyond the cervix; it is not performed if the cancer has metastasized to other areas.
Relationships:
  - Source: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: PELVIC LYMPH NODE EVALUATION
  - Description: Pelvic lymph nodes may be removed during hysterectomy
  - Source: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: Radical hysterectomy is a type of hysterectomy that removes more connective tissue and the upper vagina
  - Source: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: MODIFIED RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: A hysterectomy is a surgical procedure involving the removal of the uterus. Modified radical hysterectomy is a type of hysterectomy that, in addition to removing the uterus, also removes part of the surrounding connective tissue and a portion of the vagina. This distinguishes it from a standard hysterectomy, which typically removes only the uterus and possibly the cervix. The modified radical hysterectomy is thus a more extensive procedure than a simple hysterectomy, as it addresses additional tissue to achieve a wider margin of removal.</data>
