    <node id="MODIFIED RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Modified radical hysterectomy is a surgical procedure performed through the abdomen that involves the removal of the uterus, cervix, the parametrial tissue (the connective tissue that holds the cervix in place), and up to approximately half an inch of the upper vagina. In addition to these resections, the procedure includes lymph node evaluation, making it distinct from a simple hysterectomy. This approach is typically used for certain gynecologic cancers, as it addresses both the primary tumor and potential regional spread through lymphatic assessment.
Relationships:
  - Source: MODIFIED RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: PELVIC LYMPH NODE EVALUATION
  - Description: Pelvic lymph node evaluation is performed with modified radical hysterectomy
  - Source: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Target: MODIFIED RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: Modified radical hysterectomy is a type of hysterectomy used to treat cervical cancer
  - Source: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: MODIFIED RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: A hysterectomy is a surgical procedure involving the removal of the uterus. Modified radical hysterectomy is a type of hysterectomy that, in addition to removing the uterus, also removes part of the surrounding connective tissue and a portion of the vagina. This distinguishes it from a standard hysterectomy, which typically removes only the uterus and possibly the cervix. The modified radical hysterectomy is thus a more extensive procedure than a simple hysterectomy, as it addresses additional tissue to achieve a wider margin of removal.
  - Source: EXTRAFASCIAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: MODIFIED RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: Extrafascial hysterectomy and modified radical hysterectomy are alternative options when pre-cancer is found at cone biopsy margins</data>
