    <node id="COLOSTOMY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">COLOSTOMY is a surgical procedure and a type of ostomy in which a portion of the colon is connected to the outside of the abdomen. This creates an opening called a stoma, through which stool exits the body. Typically, the stool is collected in a bag attached to the stoma. The procedure may involve bringing the remaining part of the colon, often the upper or end section, to the abdominal wall, allowing waste to be diverted out of the body.

A colostomy can be temporary or permanent. A temporary colostomy is sometimes performed to allow the lower part of the colon or rectum to heal after surgeries such as a low anterior resection, and it can often be reversed later. A permanent colostomy is always required after an abdominoperineal resection, a procedure that removes the rectum and anus. In all cases, the colostomy effectively reroutes the normal passage of stool, preventing it from traveling through the lower colon and rectum. By creating a stoma, the colostomy provides an alternate exit for fecal matter when the usual route is diseased, damaged, or surgically removed.
Relationships:
  - Source: COLECTOMY
  - Target: COLOSTOMY
  - Description: A colectomy is the surgical removal of all or part of the colon. A colostomy is a surgical procedure that creates an opening (stoma) from the colon to the outside of the body, allowing stool to be diverted into a collection bag. A colostomy may be performed during a colectomy when it is not safe to reconnect the two healthy ends of the remaining colon. This situation can arise after cancerous tissue is removed, if the remaining sections cannot be safely rejoined. Additionally, a colectomy with colostomy is an option for treating a bowel blockage. Therefore, the combination of these two procedures is used both when rejoining the colon is unsafe and as a treatment for intestinal obstruction.
  - Source: COLOSTOMY
  - Target: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)
  - Description: Abdominoperineal resection always requires a permanent colostomy
  - Source: LOW ANTERIOR RESECTION (LAR)
  - Target: COLOSTOMY
  - Description: LAR may require a colostomy if bowel reattachment is delayed or impossible; it may be temporary or permanent
  - Source: COLORECTAL ANASTOMOSIS
  - Target: COLOSTOMY
  - Description: A colostomy may be made when colorectal anastomosis is delayed or not possible after low anterior resection
  - Source: COLOANAL ANASTOMOSIS
  - Target: COLOSTOMY
  - Description: A colostomy may be made when the colon cannot be attached to the anus after low anterior resection
  - Source: COLOSTOMY
  - Target: DOUBLE-BARRELED WET COLOSTOMY
  - Description: A double-barreled wet colostomy is a type of colostomy that handles both urine and stool through one stoma
  - Source: OSTOMY
  - Target: COLOSTOMY
  - Description: A colostomy is a type of ostomy that connects the colon to the abdominal surface
  - Source: SURGERY
  - Target: COLOSTOMY
  - Description: Colostomy is a surgical procedure that creates an opening in the abdomen; in colon cancer surgery it is usually reversed with another operation.
  - Source: COLOSTOMY
  - Target: PELVIC EXENTERATION
  - Description: If the rectum is removed during pelvic exenteration, a permanent colostomy may be created
  - Source: BOWEL BLOCKAGE
  - Target: COLOSTOMY
  - Description: Bowel blockage can be treated with a colectomy with colostomy, or a colostomy first followed by second surgery.
  - Source: TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY
  - Target: COLOSTOMY
  - Description: Some types of transabdominal surgery, such as LAR and APR, may require a colostomy</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
