      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Abdominoperineal resection (APR) is a surgical procedure used for tumors in the lower rectum; it involves removing the rectum and anus and creating a permanent colostomy.
Relationships:
  - Source: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION
  - Target: PERMANENT COLOSTOMY
  - Description: APR always involves creating a permanent colostomy for stool to leave the body
  - Source: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION
  - Target: TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY
  - Description: APR is a transabdominal surgical procedure; after the surgery the removed tissue is sent for testing
  - Source: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Chemotherapy may be a next treatment after APR for stage 2 or 3 rectal cancer
  - Source: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION
  - Target: CHEMORADIATION
  - Description: Chemoradiation may be a next treatment after APR for stage 2 or 3 rectal cancer
  - Source: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION
  - Target: SURGICAL STAGING
  - Description: The tissue and lymph nodes removed during APR are sent for surgical/pathologic staging
  - Source: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION
  - Target: RECTAL CANCER
  - Description: APR is used for tumors in the lower rectum caused by rectal cancer
  - Source: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION
  - Target: LABORATORY TESTING
  - Description: Tissue and lymph nodes removed during APR are sent to a lab for testing</data>
