    <node id="STEM CELL REMOVAL: HEALTHY STEM CELLS ARE REMOVED FROM THE PATIENT'S BLOOD OR BONE MARROW, THEN FROZEN AND STORED.">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">2. **High-dose chemotherapy:** Intensive chemotherapy is given to kill any lymphoma cells in the body; this also kills all stem cells in the bone marrow, leaving the patient vulnerable to infections and fatigued.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STEM CELL REPLACEMENT: THE HARVESTED HEALTHY STEM CELLS ARE THAWED AND RETURNED TO THE BODY TO &quot;RESCUE&quot; THE DAMAGED BONE MARROW, MIGRATING TO THE BONE MARROW AND STARTING TO GROW NEW BLOOD STEM CELLS.">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
Stem cell rescue is an intense procedure and is not for everyone with PCNSL [Data: Sources (193)]. It is only used after other treatments have been tried or if the lymphoma comes back after earlier treatment (relapse) [Data: Entities (1070); Relationships (2774, 2775, 2776); Sources (193)]. The treatment team considers factors such as fitness level, health status, organ function, cancer stage, previous treatments, and other medical conditions when deciding if stem cell rescue is appropriate [Data: Sources (193, 194)]. Because the procedure can be risky and the side effects harsh, it may not be useful for people who are older or frail [Data: Sources (194)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STEM CELL REMOVAL:">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4"> Healthy stem cells are removed from the patient's blood or bone marrow, then frozen and stored.</data>
    </node>
