    <node id="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer that originates in lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell. In this disease, the bone marrow produces an excessive number of immature lymphocytes called lymphoblasts, which crowd out healthy cells and make it difficult for the bone marrow and blood to perform their normal functions. A diagnosis of ALL requires the presence of 20% or more lymphoblasts in the bone marrow. ALL has multiple subtypes, including B-cell and T-cell ALL, which are classified based on the specific type of lymphocyte affected. It is the most common cancer diagnosed in children, though it can also affect adults. Beyond the bone marrow and blood, ALL can spread to other organs, including the testicles and the central nervous system.
Relationships:
  - Source: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: B-ALL
  - Description: Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is one of the two main types of ALL and originates in B-cell lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell responsible for producing antibodies. As a distinct subtype, B-ALL accounts for a significant proportion of ALL cases and is defined by the malignant transformation of early B-cell precursors. Thus, B-ALL is a major form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, with its cellular origin in the B-cell lineage distinguishing it from the other main type of ALL.
  - Source: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
