      <data key="d4">ENTITY: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
Description: Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer that originates in lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell. In this disease, the bone marrow produces an excessive number of immature lymphocytes called lymphoblasts, which crowd out healthy cells and make it difficult for the bone marrow and blood to perform their normal functions. A diagnosis of ALL requires the presence of 20% or more lymphoblasts in the bone marrow. ALL has multiple subtypes, including B-cell and T-cell ALL, which are classified based on the specific type of lymphocyte affected. It is the most common cancer diagnosed in children, though it can also affect adults. Beyond the bone marrow and blood, ALL can spread to other organs, including the testicles and the central nervous system.
Relationships:
  - Source: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: B-ALL
  - Description: Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is one of the two main types of ALL and originates in B-cell lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell responsible for producing antibodies. As a distinct subtype, B-ALL accounts for a significant proportion of ALL cases and is defined by the malignant transformation of early B-cell precursors. Thus, B-ALL is a major form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, with its cellular origin in the B-cell lineage distinguishing it from the other main type of ALL.
  - Source: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Description: B-cell ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) that arises from B-cell lymphocytes. It is one of the two main types of ALL, the other being T-ALL (T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia), and it is notably more common than T-ALL. B-ALL encompasses several distinct subtypes, including mature B-cell ALL and forms defined by genetic markers, such as BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL (also known as Philadelphia chromosome-positive ALL) and BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL. As a disease of B-cell precursors, B-ALL is characterized by the malignant proliferation of immature B cells in the bone marrow, which can interfere with normal blood cell production. Overall, B-ALL represents a major category of childhood and adult leukemia, and its classification into genetic subtypes is important for prognosis and treatment planning.
Relationships:
  - Source: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: B-ALL
  - Description: Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is one of the two main types of ALL and originates in B-cell lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell responsible for producing antibodies. As a distinct subtype, B-ALL accounts for a significant proportion of ALL cases and is defined by the malignant transformation of early B-cell precursors. Thus, B-ALL is a major form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, with its cellular origin in the B-cell lineage distinguishing it from the other main type of ALL.
  - Source: B-ALL
  - Target: T-ALL
  - Description: B-ALL and T-ALL are two distinct subtypes of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. Both subtypes share the same overarching disease category but arise from different lymphocyte lineages: B-ALL originates from B lymphocytes (B cells), while T-ALL originates from T lymphocytes (T cells). This lineage difference is fundamental to their biology, clinical presentation, and treatment approaches. In terms of frequency, B-ALL is more common than T-ALL, accounting for the majority of ALL cases. Together, these descriptions highlight that while B-ALL and T-ALL are related as subtypes of ALL, they are defined by their respective cellular origins, with B-ALL being the more prevalent form.
  - Source: B-ALL
  - Target: MATURE B-CELL ALL (BURKITT LEUKEMIA)
  - Description: Mature B-cell ALL, also called Burkitt leukemia, is a rare subtype of B-ALL.
  - Source: B-ALL
  - Target: BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL
  - Description: B-ALL (B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature B lymphocytes. BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL is a distinct subtype of B-ALL defined by a specific genetic abnormality: the BCR::ABL1 fusion gene, resulting from a translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22 (the Philadelphia chromosome). This genetic alteration drives the leukemia and has important implications for diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment. As a subtype of B-ALL, BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL shares the general features of B-ALL but is set apart by this molecular marker, making targeted therapies particularly relevant for this form of the disease.
  - Source: B-ALL
  - Target: BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL
  - Description: B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL) encompasses several subtypes, one of which is BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL. This subtype is defined by the absence of the BCR::ABL1 fusion gene, yet it shares a gene expression profile similar to BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL, hence the term "BCR::ABL1-like." It is recognized as a distinct subtype of B-ALL and is also the most common type of B-ALL overall. Thus, BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL represents the most frequently diagnosed form of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, characterized by its specific molecular and clinical features.
  - Source: B-ALL
  - Target: BURKITT LEUKEMIA
  - Description: Burkitt leukemia is treated differently from B-ALL.
  - Source: B-ALL
  - Target: B-CELL LYMPHOBLASTIC LYMPHOMA
  - Description: When B-cell lymphoblasts grow mostly in lymph nodes instead of bone marrow, the related condition is called B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma.
  - Source: ALL
  - Target: B-ALL
  - Description: B-ALL is a subtype of ALL that arises from B-cell lymphocytes.
  - Source: NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK
  - Target: B-ALL
  - Description: The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides clinical practice guidelines that address the treatment of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL). Specifically, the NCCN Guidelines include a dedicated chapter on B-ALL treatment, and the NCCN Guidelines for Patients also contain a chapter covering this topic. These resources are designed to help healthcare professionals and patients understand evidence-based approaches to managing B-ALL, from diagnosis through treatment planning. The NCCN thus offers comprehensive, standardized guidance on B-ALL, ensuring that both clinical practitioners and patients have access to current, expert-reviewed information on therapeutic options for this disease.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
