    <node id="ANOSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">ANOSCOPY is a medical procedure used to inspect the anal canal and lower rectum using an anoscope. It is typically performed as a surveillance exam during follow-up care for individuals treated for anal cancer, including during remission. The procedure is recommended every 6 to 12 months for a period of 3 years after treatment, when feasible. During follow-up, anoscopy is often combined with a digital rectal examination to ensure thorough assessment. In cases where treatment was limited to lymph nodes only, anoscopy is still recommended on the same schedule, every 6 to 12 months for 3 years; however, it may be omitted if the procedure causes significant pain, particularly after radiation therapy.
Relationships: (none listed in the data)</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">ENTITY: ANOSCOPY
Description: ANOSCOPY is a medical procedure used to inspect the anal canal and lower rectum using an anoscope. It is typically performed as a surveillance exam during follow-up care for individuals treated for anal cancer, including during remission. The procedure is recommended every 6 to 12 months for a period of 3 years after treatment, when feasible. During follow-up, anoscopy is often combined with a digital rectal examination to ensure thorough assessment. In cases where treatment was limited to lymph nodes only, anoscopy is still recommended on the same schedule, every 6 to 12 months for 3 years; however, it may be omitted if the procedure causes significant pain, particularly after radiation therapy.
Relationships: (none listed in the data)</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANAL DYSPLASIA">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Anal dysplasia is a precancerous condition characterized by the presence of abnormal, fast-growing cells in the anal canal or in the tissue near the anus. These lesions are considered precancerous, meaning that if left untreated, they have the potential to progress to anal cancer. Identifying and treating anal dysplasia is important because early intervention may help prevent the development of invasive anal cancer. The condition can be detected through specialized examination, particularly using high-resolution anoscopy, which allows for detailed visualization of the abnormal cellular changes. Overall, anal dysplasia represents a critical stage for medical monitoring and preventive treatment.
Relationships: None listed in the data.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
