    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK → B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides clinical practice guidelines that address the treatment of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL). Specifically, the NCCN Guidelines include a dedicated chapter on B-ALL treatment, and the NCCN Guidelines for Patients also contain a chapter covering this topic. These resources are designed to help healthcare professionals and patients understand evidence-based approaches to managing B-ALL, from diagnosis through treatment planning. The NCCN thus offers comprehensive, standardized guidance on B-ALL, ensuring that both clinical practitioners and patients have access to current, expert-reviewed information on therapeutic options for this disease.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">ENTITY: RELATIONSHIP: NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK → B-ALL
Description: The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides clinical practice guidelines that address the treatment of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL). Specifically, the NCCN Guidelines include a dedicated chapter on B-ALL treatment, and the NCCN Guidelines for Patients also contain a chapter covering this topic. These resources are designed to help healthcare professionals and patients understand evidence-based approaches to managing B-ALL, from diagnosis through treatment planning. The NCCN thus offers comprehensive, standardized guidance on B-ALL, ensuring that both clinical practitioners and patients have access to current, expert-reviewed information on therapeutic options for this disease.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA → B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is one of the two main types of ALL and originates in B-cell lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell responsible for producing antibodies. As a distinct subtype, B-ALL accounts for a significant proportion of ALL cases and is defined by the malignant transformation of early B-cell precursors. Thus, B-ALL is a major form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, with its cellular origin in the B-cell lineage distinguishing it from the other main type of ALL.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">ENTITY: RELATIONSHIP: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA → B-ALL
Description: Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is one of the two main types of ALL and originates in B-cell lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell responsible for producing antibodies. As a distinct subtype, B-ALL accounts for a significant proportion of ALL cases and is defined by the malignant transformation of early B-cell precursors. Thus, B-ALL is a major form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, with its cellular origin in the B-cell lineage distinguishing it from the other main type of ALL.</data>
    </node>
