    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: B-ALL → T-ALL">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">B-ALL and T-ALL are two distinct subtypes of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. Both subtypes share the same overarching disease category but arise from different lymphocyte lineages: B-ALL originates from B lymphocytes (B cells), while T-ALL originates from T lymphocytes (T cells). This lineage difference is fundamental to their biology, clinical presentation, and treatment approaches. In terms of frequency, B-ALL is more common than T-ALL, accounting for the majority of ALL cases. Together, these descriptions highlight that while B-ALL and T-ALL are related as subtypes of ALL, they are defined by their respective cellular origins, with B-ALL being the more prevalent form.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">ENTITY: RELATIONSHIP: B-ALL → T-ALL
Description: B-ALL and T-ALL are two distinct subtypes of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. Both subtypes share the same overarching disease category but arise from different lymphocyte lineages: B-ALL originates from B lymphocytes (B cells), while T-ALL originates from T lymphocytes (T cells). This lineage difference is fundamental to their biology, clinical presentation, and treatment approaches. In terms of frequency, B-ALL is more common than T-ALL, accounting for the majority of ALL cases. Together, these descriptions highlight that while B-ALL and T-ALL are related as subtypes of ALL, they are defined by their respective cellular origins, with B-ALL being the more prevalent form.</data>
    </node>
