    <node id="BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL">
      <data key="d3">3</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL is a distinct subtype of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL) characterized by the presence of the BCR::ABL1 gene. This gene produces the BCR::ABL protein, which drives the disease. Treatment for this subtype specifically aims to stop the activity of the BCR::ABL protein and typically involves an intensive combination of systemic therapies.
Relationships:
  - Source: B-ALL
  - Target: BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL
  - Description: B-ALL (B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature B lymphocytes. BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL is a distinct subtype of B-ALL defined by a specific genetic abnormality: the BCR::ABL1 fusion gene, resulting from a translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22 (the Philadelphia chromosome). This genetic alteration drives the leukemia and has important implications for diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment. As a subtype of B-ALL, BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL shares the general features of B-ALL but is set apart by this molecular marker, making targeted therapies particularly relevant for this form of the disease.
  - Source: BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL
  - Target: BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL
  - Description: These are distinct B-ALL subtypes classified by BCR::ABL1 status and are discussed in separate chapters of the NCCN guidelines.
  - Source: NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK
  - Target: BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL
  - Description: The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides clinical guidance that includes a dedicated chapter on the treatment of BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL. This information is presented in the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which specifically address management and treatment options for this subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
