    <node id="ILEAL CONDUIT">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">ILEAL CONDUIT is a surgical procedure and type of urinary diversion used to reroute the normal flow of urine when the bladder has been removed or is no longer functioning. In this procedure, a section of the ileum (part of the small intestine) is isolated and fashioned into a new tube-like conduit. One end of this intestinal segment is connected to the ureters, which carry urine from the kidneys, while the other end is brought to the surface of the abdomen to create a stoma. Urine then drains continuously through this stoma into an external ostomy bag worn by the patient. This method effectively bypasses the bladder, allowing urine to be collected outside the body. The ileal conduit is a common and reliable form of incontinent urinary diversion, providing a durable pathway for urine elimination when normal urinary storage and voiding are not possible.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">ENTITY: ILEAL CONDUIT
Description: ILEAL CONDUIT is a surgical procedure and type of urinary diversion used to reroute the normal flow of urine when the bladder has been removed or is no longer functioning. In this procedure, a section of the ileum (part of the small intestine) is isolated and fashioned into a new tube-like conduit. One end of this intestinal segment is connected to the ureters, which carry urine from the kidneys, while the other end is brought to the surface of the abdomen to create a stoma. Urine then drains continuously through this stoma into an external ostomy bag worn by the patient. This method effectively bypasses the bladder, allowing urine to be collected outside the body. The ileal conduit is a common and reliable form of incontinent urinary diversion, providing a durable pathway for urine elimination when normal urinary storage and voiding are not possible.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BOWEL BLOCKAGE">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">An event in which a tumor grows so large that it blocks the flow of stool; possible treatment options include one-stage colectomy, colectomy with colostomy, colostomy followed by second surgery, or stent placement followed by second surgery.
Relationships:
  - Source: BOWEL BLOCKAGE
  - Target: COLECTOMY
  - Description: A colectomy can unblock the bowel; this is called a one-stage colectomy.
  - Source: BOWEL BLOCKAGE
  - Target: COLOSTOMY
  - Description: Bowel blockage can be treated with a colectomy with colostomy, or a colostomy first followed by second surgery.</data>
