**ENTITY: BURKITT LEUKEMIA**
Description: Burkitt leukemia, also called mature B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), is a rare subtype of B-cell ALL. It is essentially the same disease as Burkitt lymphoma, sharing the same underlying pathology, but it is distinguished by its clinical presentation and treatment approach. Specifically, Burkitt leukemia is treated differently from standard B-ALL, despite being classified as a form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia. This distinction is important because, although it falls under the ALL umbrella, its management follows protocols more akin to Burkitt lymphoma rather than typical B-ALL treatment regimens. In summary, Burkitt leukemia is a rare, mature B-cell ALL that is biologically equivalent to Burkitt lymphoma yet requires a unique therapeutic strategy compared to conventional B-ALL.
Relationships:
  - Source: B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: BURKITT LEUKEMIA
  - Description: Burkitt leukemia is mature B-cell ALL, a rare form whose treatment differs from standard B-ALL. Burkitt leukemia, also called mature B-cell ALL, is treated differently from standard B-ALL.
  - Source: B-ALL
  - Target: BURKITT LEUKEMIA
  - Description: Burkitt leukemia is treated differently from B-ALL.
  - Source: B-CELL ALL
  - Target: BURKITT LEUKEMIA
  - Description: Burkitt leukemia is a rare subtype of B-cell ALL, also called mature B-cell ALL.

**ENTITY: B-CELL ALL**
Description: B-cell ALL (B-ALL) is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that starts in B-cell lymphocytes; it is more common than T-ALL.
Relationships:
  - Source: B-CELL ALL
  - Target: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Description: B-cell ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.
  - Source: B-CELL ALL
  - Target: BURKITT LEUKEMIA
  - Description: Burkitt leukemia is a rare subtype of B-cell ALL, also called mature B-cell ALL.

**ENTITY: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA**
Description: Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer that originates in lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell. In this disease, the bone marrow produces an excessive number of immature lymphocytes called lymphoblasts, which crowd out healthy cells and make it difficult for the bone marrow and blood to perform their normal functions. A diagnosis of ALL requires the presence of 20% or more lymphoblasts in the bone marrow. ALL has multiple subtypes, including B-cell and T-cell ALL, which are classified based on the specific type of lymphocyte affected. It is the most common cancer diagnosed in children, though it can also affect adults. Beyond the bone marrow and blood, ALL can spread to other organs, including the testicles and the central nervous system.
Relationships:
  - Source: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: B-ALL
  - Description: Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is one of the two main types of ALL and originates in B-cell lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell responsible for producing antibodies. As a distinct subtype, B-ALL accounts for a significant proportion of ALL cases and is defined by the malignant transformation of early B-cell precursors. Thus, B-ALL is a major form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, with its cellular origin in the B-cell lineage distinguishing it from the other main type of ALL.
  - Source: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Description: B-cell ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.

**ENTITY: T-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA**
Description: T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL) is a subtype of ALL that starts in T-cell lymphocytes. It can cause an enlarged thymus, which may lead to breathing problems. When T-cell lymphoblasts grow mostly in the thymus instead of the bone marrow, it is called T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma.
Relationships:
  - Source: B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: T-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Description: B-cell ALL is more common than T-cell ALL.

**ENTITY: T-ALL**
Description: T-ALL, the acronym for T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) that originates in T-cell lymphocytes. As one of the two main types of ALL, it is less common than B-ALL. This condition may cause an enlarged thymus. Treatment options for T-ALL include participation in a clinical trial or chemotherapy.
Relationships:
  - Source: B-ALL
  - Target: T-ALL
  - Description: B-ALL and T-ALL are two distinct subtypes of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. Both subtypes share the same overarching disease category but arise from different lymphocyte lineages: B-ALL originates from B lymphocytes (B cells), while T-ALL originates from T lymphocytes (T cells). This lineage difference is fundamental to their biology, clinical presentation, and treatment approaches. In terms of frequency, B-ALL is more common than T-ALL, accounting for the majority of ALL cases. Together, these descriptions highlight that while B-ALL and T-ALL are related as subtypes of ALL, they are defined by their respective cellular origins, with B-ALL being the more prevalent form.

**ENTITY: T-CELL ALL**
Description: T-cell ALL (T-ALL) is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that starts in T-cell lymphocytes and can cause an enlarged thymus.
Relationships: None listed in the data.

