    <edge source="SURGERY" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surgery and radiation therapy are two established cancer treatment modalities that can be used alone, together, or in sequence. Both are recognized treatment options for cancers such as thyroid cancer and squamous cell skin cancer. Their specific relationship depends on the clinical situation.

Radiation therapy can be given before or after surgery. When given before surgery, it may help shrink a tumor before surgical removal. When given after surgery, it is often used to eliminate remaining cancer cells. In particular, radiation therapy is an option if a concerning amount of cancer remains after surgery. This makes radiation therapy an important complement to surgery, especially when surgery alone may not be sufficient.

Radiation therapy is also used as an alternative to surgery. For patients who cannot undergo surgery, or who choose not to have surgery, radiation therapy can be used instead. Therefore, while surgery and radiation therapy are sometimes combined, they can also be competing options. The descriptions are consistent: radiation therapy may be used before or after surgery; it may be added after surgery if cancer remains; it is listed as another treatment option for thyroid cancer alongside surgery; and it is a treatment option for squamous cell skin cancer along with surgery.

Ultimately, the choice between surgery and radiation therapyor a decision to use bothdepends on the type and stage of cancer, the patients overall health, whether surgery is feasible, and patient preference. In some cases, surgery is the primary treatment, with radiation therapy used as a follow-up or backup. In other cases, radiation therapy is the primary treatment because surgery cannot be performed or is declined. Together, surgery and radiation therapy provide flexible, potentially curative, or disease-controlling options across multiple cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d8">SURGERY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="CHEMORADIATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surgery and chemoradiation are two alternative treatment approaches for perianal cancer. For stage 1B3 and 2A2 cancers, chemoradiation is generally preferred over surgery as the primary treatment modality. However, surgery is sometimes recommended depending on individual patient circumstances or clinical considerations, meaning that while chemoradiation holds a preferential status for these specific stages, surgery remains a viable option in select cases.</data>
      <data key="d8">SURGERY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="OVARIAN CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surgery is a primary and often initial treatment for ovarian cancer. It serves two key purposes: removing the cancerous tissue and determining the extent of the disease, a process known as staging. By surgically removing ovarian cancer, doctors aim to eliminate as much of the tumor as possible, which is essential for effective treatment. Additionally, the information gathered during surgery helps assess how far the cancer has spread, guiding subsequent treatment decisions. Thus, surgery plays a dual role in both treating and evaluating ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">SURGERY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surgery and cancer treatment are closely interconnected, with surgery serving as a major therapeutic approach in oncology. Surgery is a form of cancer treatment that is used to remove cancerous tissue from the body. It is also one component of a broader cancer treatment plan, which may include other modalities such as radiation therapy, chemotherapy, or immunotherapy. In the context of head and neck cancers specifically, surgery is considered a complex form of cancer treatment due to the intricate anatomy of the region and the need for precise removal of tumors while preserving critical functions like breathing, swallowing, and speech. Additionally, discussions of surgery as a cancer treatment include attention to surgery side effects in cancer care, acknowledging that the procedure can have physical and functional impacts that must be managed before, during, and after treatment. Thus, surgery is both a distinct, direct method for excising cancer and an integral part of multidisciplinary cancer care, with its own risks and benefits that are weighed carefully by the medical team.</data>
      <data key="d8">SURGERY</data>
    </edge>
