    <edge source="NCCN" target="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN is an organization that publishes clinical guidelines and provides resources related to cancer care. Among its resources is the NCCN Cancer Centers network, which consists of cancer centers affiliated with NCCN. These NCCN Cancer Centers are listed on the NCCN website, specifically at NCCN.org/cancercenters, and serve as a clinical trial resource. The list is designed to help patients and providers find cancer centers and available clinical trials in the United States. NCCN Cancer Centers are therefore an integral part of NCCNs effort to connect individuals with cancer treatment and research opportunities, while NCCN itself is recognized for its clinical guidelines and its role in maintaining this network of affiliated cancer centers.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="CANCER.NET" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients list Cancer.Net as a resource.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) and the National Cancer Institute (NCI) are both prominent cancer-focused organizations that serve as authoritative resources for cancer information, clinical trials, and treatment guidance. Both entities are consistently identified in the source material as key resources for patients and providers seeking to find clinical trials and obtain guidance on cancer treatment. NCCN is recognized for providing patient guidelines as well as information about cancer centers, offering practical, structured guidance to help patients understand their treatment options. NCI is recognized for providing clinical trial search capabilities, enabling patients, caregivers, and researchers to identify relevant ongoing studies. Together, they offer complementary resources within the cancer information landscape: NCCN contributes trusted patient-facing guidelines and a network of cancer centers, while NCI contributes a robust clinical trial search tool. Both are listed as cancer-focused organizations that patients can consult when seeking information about clinical trials and cancer treatment guidance.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
