      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Metastatic anal cancer is treated with platinum-based chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CHEMORADIATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a cancer treatment that uses drugs to kill or slow the growth of cancer cells. Chemoradiation is a treatment approach that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy. In this combined regimen, chemotherapy serves as one of the core components, specifically functioning as the chemotherapy portion of the treatment. Chemoradiation may be followed by additional chemotherapy, which is given to kill any lingering cancer cells that remain after the initial combined treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="OVARIAN CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is one of the main treatments for ovarian cancer. In cases where ovarian cancer has spread too extensively for initial surgery, chemotherapy is needed first.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a type of cancer treatment and one component of the broader category of cancer treatment. It is used to combat cancer but can have side effects, notably affecting fertility. Thus, while chemotherapy is a key part of cancer treatment, it is not the entirety of it, and its impact on fertility is an important consideration for patients.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a form of systemic therapy. Systemic therapy is a treatment approach that uses substances that travel through the bloodstream, reaching and affecting cells throughout the entire body rather than targeting a single, localized area. Chemotherapy specifically involves the use of cytotoxic drugs to destroy rapidly dividing cells, most commonly cancer cells, by interfering with their ability to grow and multiply. Because chemotherapy is a systemic therapy, it can treat cancer that has spread to multiple sites in the body, unlike localized treatments such as surgery or radiation therapy, which focus on a specific region. In summary, chemotherapy and systemic therapy are closely related: chemotherapy is one specific type of systemic therapy, and systemic therapy is the broader category encompassing treatments that work throughout the body.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a main treatment for primary CNS lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cervical cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the cervix. Chemotherapy is a treatment option for cervical cancer, and it is also used as part of a broader treatment approach for this condition. In some cases, chemotherapy may be the primary treatment, while in others it is combined with other therapies such as surgery or radiation. Thus, chemotherapy plays a role both as a standalone option and as a component of a multidisciplinary treatment plan for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a treatment for head and neck cancer that can affect fertility</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="PATIENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy can affect the patient's fertility</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a form of medical treatment used primarily in oncology. As a cancer treatment, chemotherapy involves the use of cytotoxic drugs to destroy or inhibit the growth of cancer cells. It is applicable across multiple cancer types and is often integrated into broader therapeutic protocols. Specifically, chemotherapy is a recognized type of treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where it serves as the standard-of-care approach for managing this blood cancer. In addition to its role in AML, chemotherapy is also a component of cervical cancer treatment, frequently administered as part of chemoradiationa combination of chemotherapy and radiation therapy that enhances the effectiveness of treatment. As such, chemotherapy occupies a central position within the overall category of cancer treatment, functioning both as a standalone modality and as an adjunct to other therapies. The entity TREATMENT encompasses a wide range of medical interventions, and chemotherapy is one of its key subtypes, distinguished by its mechanism of action and its established role in hematologic and solid tumor malignancies. Overall, chemotherapy exemplifies a targeted and evidence-based treatment option, with particular significance in AML, where it is the established standard of care, and in cervical cancer, where it is integrated into combined-modality regimens.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy and radiation therapy are two treatment modalities that are frequently combined to enhance effectiveness, particularly in oncology. When used together, the combination is referred to as chemoradiation. Chemotherapy may be administered as part of recommended treatment regimens alongside radiation therapy to treat anal cancer. Additionally, chemotherapy may be given in addition to radiation for recurrent inguinal lymph nodes, with chemoradiation combining both modalities. Radiation therapy, for its part, may be delivered alone or in combination with chemotherapy, and can also be combined with systemic therapy in certain situations. In all cases, the combined use of chemotherapy and radiation therapytermed chemoradiationrepresents a coordinated approach in which the two treatments complement each other, whether the goal is curative treatment for anal cancer or management of recurrent disease in inguinal lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">SURGERY and CHEMOTHERAPY are two primary treatment modalities used for ovarian cancer, and a gynecologic oncologist is an expert in both. These treatments are often combined in a coordinated sequence, and the order in which they are administered depends on the extent of the cancer at diagnosis.

CHEMOTHERAPY may be given before SURGERY, in which case it is referred to as neoadjuvant therapy. This approach is typically used when the cancer has spread too extensively to be removed safely or completely by initial SURGERY. By shrinking the tumors first, neoadjuvant chemotherapy can make subsequent surgical removal more feasible and effective. Alternatively, CHEMOTHERAPY may be given after SURGERY, known as primary or adjuvant chemotherapy. This is used to eliminate any remaining cancer cells that may not have been visible or removable during the operation. Adjuvant chemotherapy is recommended after SURGERY for most stage 1 ovarian cancers, as well as for stages 2, 3, and 4.

In summary, SURGERY and CHEMOTHERAPY are both essential and complementary tools in treating ovarian cancer. Their optimal sequence is determined by disease stage and resectability: if SURGERY cannot be performed first due to extensive spread, CHEMOTHERAPY is administered before SURGERY; otherwise, SURGERY is performed first and followed by CHEMOTHERAPY. The combined expertise of a gynecologic oncologist covers both modalities, ensuring that patients receive the most appropriate and individualized treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="NCCN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides supportive-care information and patient guidelines that are directly relevant to individuals undergoing chemotherapy. In its patient education materials, the NCCN describes chemotherapy as a type of systemic therapy, meaning it travels through the bloodstream to treat cancer cells throughout the body. Together, these descriptions highlight the NCCNs dual role in both educating patients about the nature of chemotherapy and offering practical guidance for managing treatment-related care.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
