      <data key="d7">Vomiting is a common side effect of chemotherapy regimens used for anal cancer, as noted in the source text. (This relationship is supported by the source text, though it is not explicitly listed as a separate relationship in the data tables.)</data>
      <data key="d8">VOMITING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="FATIGUE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Fatigue is associated with high-dose chemotherapy. (This relationship is supported by the entity description, though it is not explicitly listed as a separate relationship in the data tables.)</data>
      <data key="d8">FATIGUE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="SIDE EFFECTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy can cause side effects including nausea, vomiting, fatigue, fever, infection, tingling or numbness, rash, and diarrhea, as well as effects on fertility. (This relationship is supported by the entity description, though it is not explicitly listed as a separate relationship in the data tables.)</data>
      <data key="d8">SIDE EFFECTS</data>
    </edge>
