      <data key="d7">A genetic counselor helps patients decide about germline testing and interprets results.</data>
      <data key="d8">GERMLINE TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC COUNSELOR" target="GENETIC COUNSELING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A genetic counselor is the professional who provides genetic counseling</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC COUNSELOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIMARY CARE PHYSICIAN" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The primary care physician is part of the care team and should be kept informed about changes to the contact list</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CARE PHYSICIAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIMARY CARE PHYSICIAN" target="PATIENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient should keep their primary care physician informed of care team changes and regard the PCP as a partner in their care.</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CARE PHYSICIAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIMARY CARE PHYSICIAN" target="ANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Primary care physicians are often the first health care professionals patients with anal symptoms consult</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CARE PHYSICIAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HIGH-RESOLUTION ANOSCOPY" target="HEALTH CARE PROFESSIONAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Health care providers trained in high-resolution anoscopy conduct the procedure</data>
      <data key="d8">HEALTH CARE PROFESSIONAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HIGH-RESOLUTION ANOSCOPY" target="STANDARD ANOSCOPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">High-resolution anoscopy may be more effective than standard anoscopy at identifying anal cancer and anal dysplasia</data>
      <data key="d8">STANDARD ANOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ILLNESS OR INJURY" target="PATIENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient should list any past illness or injury in their medical history.</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TEST" target="MEDICAL PROVIDERS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Medical providers can discuss and order genetic testing.</data>
      <data key="d8">MEDICAL PROVIDERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TEST" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team puts together all test results, including genetic tests, to determine the level of prostate cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TEST" target="PROSTATE CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A genetic test finds abnormal gene changes associated with inherited risk for cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TEST" target="MUTATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A genetic test is used to find abnormal changes (mutations) in genes.</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TEST" target="GERMLINE TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">GENETIC TEST is a broad category of medical analysis used to identify changes in chromosomes, genes, or proteins. GERMLINE TESTING is a specific form of GENETIC TEST that focuses on inherited cancer risk, meaning it looks for genetic mutations present from birth that may predispose an individual to certain cancers. In the context of prostate cancer care, GERMLINE TESTING is recognized as one of the two basic types of genetic tests used, alongside somatic testing (which examines tumor tissue). As such, GERMLINE TESTING serves both to assess hereditary cancer susceptibility and to guide treatment decisions for prostate cancer, making it a key tool in precision oncology.</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TEST" target="GENETIC COUNSELOR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A genetic counselor helps patients decide on and interpret genetic tests.</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TEST" target="MOLECULAR BIOMARKER TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Molecular biomarker testing is one of the two basic types of genetic tests used for prostate cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d8">MOLECULAR BIOMARKER TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PHARMACIST" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Pharmacists prepare and dispense chemotherapy medicines and explain their proper use and side effects</data>
      <data key="d8">PHARMACIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PHARMACIST" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team may include pharmacists</data>
      <data key="d8">PHARMACIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INFECTIONS" target="FAMILY HISTORY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Family history questions include illnesses, infections, and injuries.</data>
      <data key="d8">INFECTIONS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEDICAL HISTORY" target="FAMILY HISTORY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Medical history and family history are two related but distinct health histories used by the care team. Family history is a specific component of the broader medical history. While the medical history encompasses a patients overall health background, the family history focuses specifically on the health conditions and patterns that occur among blood relatives. Both are important for guiding clinical care, but family history contributes to the medical history by providing insight into inherited risks and familial health trends.</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILY HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
