    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The NCCN Cancer Centers serve as a key resource for locating clinical trials, specifically within the United States. These centers assist patients in identifying and accessing clinical trial opportunities, making them a valuable source of information for those seeking participation in research studies. The entity CLINICAL TRIAL is central to this resource, as the primary function of the NCCN Cancer Centers is to connect individuals with relevant clinical trials. In summary, NCCN Cancer Centers provide a United Statesbased resource dedicated to helping patients find clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCI provides a portal for patients to search for cancer clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="NCI'S CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CIS helps patients who need help finding a clinical trial.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCI'S CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Metastatic disease affects treatment planning and may be classified as very advanced disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemoradiation is a treatment modality that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy. Specifically, chemoradiation includes chemotherapy administered concurrently with radiation therapy, meaning the drug component is given at the same time as the radiation treatment. In the context of external beam radiation therapy (EBRT), chemotherapy serves as the drug component delivered concurrently with EBRT as part of the chemoradiation regimen. The available descriptions indicate that chemoradiation, chemotherapy, or both may be listed as possible next treatment options for a patient, depending on the clinical scenario. Chemotherapy, as the drug component, works in conjunction with radiation to enhance the overall treatment effect. Thus, chemoradiation represents the integration of systemic drug therapy and localized radiation, while chemotherapy itself can also be considered independently as a treatment option.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMORADIATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="BRACHYTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Brachytherapy is recommended in addition to chemoradiation for most locally advanced cervical cancers</data>
      <data key="d8">BRACHYTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemoradiation is a type of treatment for cervical cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="LOCAL EXCISION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Local excision may be used as an alternative to chemoradiation for some anal cancers. Specifically, it can be considered instead of chemoradiation for certain slow-growing stage I and II perianal cancers. Thus, for select cases, local excision serves as a treatment option in place of chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d8">LOCAL EXCISION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="LOCALLY ADVANCED CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemoradiation is recommended for most locally advanced cervical cancers.</data>
      <data key="d8">LOCALLY ADVANCED CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="ANAL CANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemoradiation is the recommended primary treatment for non-metastatic anal canal cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="APR SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">APR surgery may be needed when anal cancer does not respond to initial treatment such as chemoradiation or returns after it</data>
      <data key="d8">APR SURGERY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a specific type of systemic therapy. As a systemic therapy, chemotherapy is administered intravenously and travels through the bloodstream, allowing it to reach cells throughout the body rather than targeting only one localized area. This broad distribution is a defining characteristic of systemic treatment. Chemotherapy is also one of the most common types of systemic therapy used for bladder cancer. Thus, while systemic therapy is the broader category encompassing various treatment approaches that affect the whole body, chemotherapy is a prominent and widely utilized form of that category, particularly in oncology.</data>
      <data key="d8">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="TARGETED THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Targeted therapy is a type of systemic therapy</data>
      <data key="d8">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
