    <edge source="MRI SCAN" target="DIAGNOSTIC TESTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MRI scans are diagnostic imaging tests</data>
      <data key="d8">DIAGNOSTIC TESTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI SCAN" target="PET SCAN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MRI SCAN (Magnetic Resonance Imaging) and PET SCAN (Positron Emission Tomography) are two distinct imaging methods that can be combined into a single PET/MRI scanning procedure. In this combined approach, the PET scan and MRI scan work together to provide a more detailed and comprehensive image than either modality could offer alone. The PET component is particularly useful for identifying areas of abnormal metabolic activity, which helps determine where cancer cells are located. Meanwhile, the MRI component supplies high-resolution anatomical detail of soft tissues. By integrating both technologies, PET/MRI scanning enables clinicians to correlate functional information from the PET scan with structural information from the MRI scan, leading to more accurate diagnosis and localization of disease. Thus, MRI SCAN and PET SCAN are complementary imaging tools, and their combined use in PET/MRI scanning enhances the ability to pinpoint cancerous sites and assess the extent of disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">PET SCAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI SCAN" target="RECURRENCE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MRI scans may be used after treatment to check whether cancer has come back or recurred</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
