    <edge source="MYCHART" target="PHYSICIAN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MyChart (the patient portal) messages are not immediately seen by a physician. Because of this, any urgent concerns should not be sent through MyChart or portal messaging; instead, they should be called directly to triage. Physicians do not monitor MyChart messages in real time, so portal messaging is not appropriate for time-sensitive or urgent medical issues.</data>
      <data key="d8">PHYSICIAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MYCHART" target="PATIENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MyChart is a patient portal that allows patients to access test results, track appointments, and communicate with their care team. A patient may set up MyChart specifically to track appointments and communicate with their care team, thereby gaining access to these features and maintaining ongoing engagement with their healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MYCHART" target="NURSE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MyChart messages are not immediately seen by a nurse. Because portal messages are not monitored in real time, urgent concerns should not be sent through MyChart. Instead, patients should call triage directly for immediate assistance.</data>
      <data key="d8">NURSE</data>
    </edge>
