    <edge source="COLECTOMY" target="IMMUNOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Checkpoint inhibitor immunotherapy is recommended before colectomy for dMMR/MSI-H cancers.</data>
      <data key="d8">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLECTOMY" target="COLOSTOMY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A colectomy is the surgical removal of all or part of the colon. A colostomy is a surgical procedure that creates an opening (stoma) from the colon to the outside of the body, allowing stool to be diverted into a collection bag. A colostomy may be performed during a colectomy when it is not safe to reconnect the two healthy ends of the remaining colon. This situation can arise after cancerous tissue is removed, if the remaining sections cannot be safely rejoined. Additionally, a colectomy with colostomy is an option for treating a bowel blockage. Therefore, the combination of these two procedures is used both when rejoining the colon is unsafe and as a treatment for intestinal obstruction.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLOSTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLECTOMY" target="ANASTOMOTIC LEAK" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Anastomotic leak is a possible side effect of colectomy, occurring at the reconnection site.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLECTOMY" target="SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Colectomy is a surgical procedure performed to treat colon cancer by removing cancerous tissue from the colon. As a colon cancer surgery, it serves as the primary intervention for excising malignant growths. Colectomy can be performed in several ways: as a one-stage procedure, in which the diseased portion is removed and the colon is immediately reconnected; with a colostomy, where the end of the intestine is brought through the abdominal wall to divert waste; or after stenting, a technique used to open a blocked colon prior to surgery, allowing for a safer or delayed operation. Following the colectomy, critical medical decisions regarding further treatment and surveillance recommendations are made. These post-surgical choices are based on the pathology results and the extent of cancer removal. Thus, colectomy not only removes the cancer but also serves as a pivotal point for determining the patient's ongoing care plan, including whether additional therapies such as chemotherapy or radiation are needed, and how frequently the patient should be monitored for recurrence. In summary, colectomy is both a curative surgical intervention and a foundational step in the management of colon cancer, guiding subsequent clinical decisions and long-term follow-up strategies.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLECTOMY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Colectomy is a surgical procedure to remove part or all of the colon, often performed to treat colon cancer. Chemotherapy is a systemic treatment that uses drugs to destroy cancer cells. In the context of colon cancer treatment, chemotherapy is commonly administered after a colectomy for certain stages of the disease. It is not recommended after colectomy for stage 1 cancers, as the risk of recurrence is low and the benefits do not outweigh the potential side effects. For other, more advanced stages, chemotherapy may be given following surgery to eliminate any remaining cancer cells and reduce the risk of recurrence. Thus, while postoperative chemotherapy is a standard option for appropriate stages, its use is avoided in stage 1 cases.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLECTOMY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY AFTER SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Pathology results from the colectomy assign a cancer stage that helps determine whether chemotherapy after surgery is needed.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLECTOMY" target="HERNIA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Hernia is a possible short- or long-term side effect of colectomy, caused by organs pushing through weakened tissues.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
