      <data key="d8">UNITED STATES</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN FOUNDATION" target="PFIZER INC." id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Pfizer Inc. is a corporate supporter acknowledged by the NCCN Foundation for helping to make available the NCCN Guidelines for Patients.
Pfizer Inc. is a corporate supporter acknowledged by the NCCN Foundation for helping make the NCCN Guidelines for Patients available.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN FOUNDATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANAL CANCER FOUNDATION" target="PEER-TO-PEER SUPPORT PROGRAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Anal Cancer Foundation runs the Peer-To-Peer Support Program and provides contact for matching patients/caregivers with peer volunteers.</data>
      <data key="d8">PEER-TO-PEER SUPPORT PROGRAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANAL CANCER FOUNDATION" target="NCCN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text lists the NCCN Guidelines and the Anal Cancer Foundations Peer-To-Peer Support Program as support resources for cancer survivors</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANCER FOUNDATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANAL CANCER FOUNDATION" target="THOM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Thom is an anal cancer survivor quoted in the same survivorship resource that also presents Anal Cancer Foundation support services</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANCER FOUNDATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="UNITED STATES" target="HPV VACCINATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The 9-strain HPV vaccine is available in the United States and vaccination is an option for adults aged 45 and under.</data>
      <data key="d8">HPV VACCINATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="UNITED STATES" target="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN Cancer Centers are located in the United States.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" target="CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) operates the Cancer Information Service (CIS), which is an integral part of the Institute. The CIS functions as NCI's dedicated service for providing cancer information and clinical trial assistance. It helps both patients and the general public find relevant clinical trials and offers detailed information about clinical trial options. As a core component of the National Cancer Institute, the Cancer Information Service serves as a reliable resource for those seeking guidance on cancer-related topics and access to clinical trial information.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" target="NCI'S CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Cancer Information Service is part of the National Cancer Institute and helps people find clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) provides comprehensive clinical trial information and search resources. These resources are available through the NCI's official website at cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/clinical-trials/search, where patients, caregivers, and researchers can locate ongoing clinical trials. In addition to the online search tool, the National Cancer Institute's Cancer Information Service is also available to help individuals find clinical trials that may be relevant to their needs. Together, these offerings ensure that the public has access to both self-service search tools and personalized assistance when seeking information about clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) serves as a key resource for patients and others seeking information about clinical trials. As part of its mission, NCI provides a dedicated clinical-trials search tool on its official website, cancer.gov, enabling users to find relevant studies. In addition to this search functionality, NCI actively supports patients throughout the process of identifying and accessing clinical trials, reinforcing its role as a trusted resource in cancer research and care.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" target="UNITED STATES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a U.S. government organization operating within the United States. It is formally a U.S. government agency and institute, and it is located in the United States. The NCI is also listed under "In the United States" as a resource for finding clinical trials, reflecting its role in providing access to cancer research and treatment information. As a government entity, the NCI operates as part of the U.S. federal structure, dedicated to cancer research and related public health efforts.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" target="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) and the U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) are both U.S. government health organizations and official resources for finding clinical trials. NCI provides cancer-specific clinical trial search tools, including access through cancer.gov, while NLM maintains ClinicalTrials.gov, a comprehensive database of clinical studies. Together, these two entities serve as key sources for patients, researchers, and the public seeking information on clinical trials, with NCI focusing on cancer-related trials and NLM offering a broader clinical trial registry. Both are listed as reliable, government-supported resources for clinical-trial searching.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" target="U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The FDA and NCI are U.S. government agencies involved in cancer treatment and clinical research; the FDA approves treatments while NCI provides trial resources.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" target="CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE (NLM) and the CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE (CIS) are both organizations that help people find clinical trials. NLM maintains ClinicalTrials.gov, a comprehensive online database of clinical studies, while CIS offers direct, personal assistance to individuals seeking information about clinical trial options. Together, they provide complementary support: NLM supplies the searchable public resource, and CIS delivers one-on-one guidance to help users navigate the process.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE (NCI)" target="U.S." id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Cancer Institute is a U.S. national institute, as indicated by its cancer.gov government website.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE (NCI)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIMARY LIVER CANCER" target="LIVER CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Primary liver cancer is cancer that starts in the liver, meaning it is a specific category of liver cancer. It is distinct from cancers that spread to the liver from other parts of the body. As a type of liver cancer, primary liver cancer originates within the liver itself, and it falls under the broader classification of liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">LIVER CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIMARY LIVER CANCER" target="HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA (HCC)" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">HCC is the most common type of primary liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY LIVER CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIMARY LIVER CANCER" target="HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Hepatocellular carcinoma is the most common type of primary liver cancer in adults</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY LIVER CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIMARY LIVER CANCER" target="MIXED-TYPE TUMOR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A mixed-type tumor is a rare type of primary liver cancer containing both hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY LIVER CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIMARY LIVER CANCER" target="INTRAHEPATIC CHOLANGIOCARCINOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma is the second most common type of primary liver cancer in adults</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY LIVER CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIMARY LIVER CANCER" target="SARCOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Rare sarcomas that start in blood vessel cells are a type of primary liver cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY LIVER CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SECONDARY LIVER CANCER" target="LIVER CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Secondary liver cancer is a form of liver cancer that occurs when cancer cells from another part of the body spread, or metastasize, to the liver. It is not a cancer that originates in the liver itself, but rather a secondary site of a primary cancer located elsewhere. As such, secondary liver cancer is categorized under the broader term "liver cancer," which encompasses both primary liver cancer (starting in the liver) and secondary liver cancer (spreading to the liver from other organs). The development of secondary liver cancer indicates that the original cancer has advanced and disseminated through the bloodstream or lymphatic system. Treatment and prognosis depend on the source of the primary cancer, the extent of liver involvement, and overall patient health.</data>
      <data key="d8">LIVER CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SECONDARY LIVER CANCER" target="METASTATIC COLON CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Metastatic colon cancer to the liver is given as an example of secondary liver cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">SECONDARY LIVER CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTATIC LIVER CANCER" target="LIVER CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Liver cancer can spread to distant sites such as the lung or bone, becoming metastatic liver cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">LIVER CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA" target="MIXED-TYPE TUMOR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A mixed-type tumor is made in part of hepatocellular carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA" target="FIBROLAMELLAR HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Fibrolamellar hepatocellular carcinoma is a subtype of hepatocellular carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEREOTACTIC BODY RADIATION THERAPY" target="LIVER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">SBRT delivers high-dose radiation to tumors in the liver.</data>
      <data key="d8">LIVER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTATIC CERVICAL CANCER" target="LIVER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Metastatic cervical cancer can spread to the liver.</data>
      <data key="d8">LIVER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRACHYTHERAPY" target="VAGINAL BRACHYTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Brachytherapy is a form of radiation therapy in which a radioactive source is placed inside or near the area requiring treatment. Vaginal brachytherapy is a specific type of brachytherapy that is applied directly to the vagina. In this procedure, a radioactive device is temporarily placed within the vaginal canal to deliver targeted radiation to the surrounding tissues. Vaginal brachytherapy is used to treat certain gynecological conditions, most commonly as part of the management of endometrial or vaginal cancers, often following surgery or external beam radiation. Because the radiation is delivered locally, it helps spare healthy surrounding organs from unnecessary exposure. Thus, vaginal brachytherapy is a subtype of the broader brachytherapy modality, distinguished by its anatomical site of delivery.</data>
      <data key="d8">BRACHYTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRACHYTHERAPY" target="INTRACAVITARY BRACHYTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Intracavitary brachytherapy is a method of brachytherapy in which instruments are placed in the cervix, uterus, and vagina.</data>
      <data key="d8">BRACHYTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRACHYTHERAPY" target="INTERSTITIAL BRACHYTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Interstitial brachytherapy is a specialized technique of brachytherapy for advanced cancers involving the parametrium.</data>
      <data key="d8">BRACHYTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRACHYTHERAPY" target="CERVIX" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Brachytherapy instruments are placed into the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d8">BRACHYTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRACHYTHERAPY" target="UTERUS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Brachytherapy instruments are placed into the uterus.</data>
      <data key="d8">BRACHYTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRACHYTHERAPY" target="VAGINA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Brachytherapy instruments are placed into the vagina.</data>
      <data key="d8">BRACHYTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRACHYTHERAPY" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Brachytherapy is used to treat cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">BRACHYTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRACHYTHERAPY" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Brachytherapy is a type of treatment for cervical cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">BRACHYTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRACHYTHERAPY" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Brachytherapy is a type of radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d8">BRACHYTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOCAL EXCISION" target="RE-EXCISION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Re-excision is the preferred next treatment if local excision leaves a margin of less than 1 cm or a margin containing cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d8">LOCAL EXCISION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOCAL EXCISION" target="CHEMORADIATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemoradiation is recommended for nonmetastatic perianal cancers that do not meet the criteria for local excision.</data>
      <data key="d8">LOCAL EXCISION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOCAL EXCISION" target="SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Local excision is a surgical procedure, also referred to as surgery.</data>
      <data key="d8">LOCAL EXCISION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOCAL EXCISION" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy, with or without chemotherapy, is an option when additional treatment is needed after local excision.</data>
      <data key="d8">LOCAL EXCISION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOCAL EXCISION" target="CANCER STAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cancer staging determines whether local excision is an option, since it is recommended only for certain stage I and IIA cancers.</data>
      <data key="d8">LOCAL EXCISION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY (EBRT)" target="VAGINAL BRACHYTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Vaginal brachytherapy may be given in addition to EBRT</data>
      <data key="d8">VAGINAL BRACHYTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOCALLY ADVANCED CERVICAL CANCER" target="CERVIX" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Locally advanced cervical cancer has grown beyond the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d8">LOCALLY ADVANCED CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOCAL RECURRENCE" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Very high-risk CSCC has an increased risk of both local recurrence and metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d8">LOCAL RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOCAL RECURRENCE" target="PERINEURAL INVASION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
