    <edge source="ALL" target="B-ALL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">B-ALL is a subtype of ALL that arises from B-cell lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d8">B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="B-ALL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is one of the two main types of ALL and originates in B-cell lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell responsible for producing antibodies. As a distinct subtype, B-ALL accounts for a significant proportion of ALL cases and is defined by the malignant transformation of early B-cell precursors. Thus, B-ALL is a major form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, with its cellular origin in the B-cell lineage distinguishing it from the other main type of ALL.</data>
      <data key="d8">B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">B-cell ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d8">B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="BURKITT LEUKEMIA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Burkitt leukemia, also known as mature B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), is a rare subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia. It is a distinct form of the disease, categorized under the broader entity of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, and is recognized for its mature B-cell phenotype.</data>
      <data key="d8">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="T-ALL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">B-ALL and T-ALL are two distinct subtypes of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. Both subtypes share the same overarching disease category but arise from different lymphocyte lineages: B-ALL originates from B lymphocytes (B cells), while T-ALL originates from T lymphocytes (T cells). This lineage difference is fundamental to their biology, clinical presentation, and treatment approaches. In terms of frequency, B-ALL is more common than T-ALL, accounting for the majority of ALL cases. Together, these descriptions highlight that while B-ALL and T-ALL are related as subtypes of ALL, they are defined by their respective cellular origins, with B-ALL being the more prevalent form.</data>
      <data key="d8">B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="BURKITT LEUKEMIA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Burkitt leukemia is treated differently from B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d8">B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="B-CELL LYMPHOBLASTIC LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">When B-cell lymphoblasts grow mostly in lymph nodes instead of bone marrow, the related condition is called B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d8">B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="MATURE B-CELL ALL (BURKITT LEUKEMIA)" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Mature B-cell ALL, also called Burkitt leukemia, is a rare subtype of B-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d8">B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">B-ALL (B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature B lymphocytes. BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL is a distinct subtype of B-ALL defined by a specific genetic abnormality: the BCR::ABL1 fusion gene, resulting from a translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22 (the Philadelphia chromosome). This genetic alteration drives the leukemia and has important implications for diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment. As a subtype of B-ALL, BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL shares the general features of B-ALL but is set apart by this molecular marker, making targeted therapies particularly relevant for this form of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL) encompasses several subtypes, one of which is BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL. This subtype is defined by the absence of the BCR::ABL1 fusion gene, yet it shares a gene expression profile similar to BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL, hence the term "BCR::ABL1-like." It is recognized as a distinct subtype of B-ALL and is also the most common type of B-ALL overall. Thus, BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL represents the most frequently diagnosed form of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, characterized by its specific molecular and clinical features.</data>
      <data key="d8">B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="B-ALL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">B-ALL is the acronym for B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d8">B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="BURKITT LEUKEMIA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Burkitt leukemia is mature B-cell ALL, a rare form whose treatment differs from standard B-ALL. Burkitt leukemia, also called mature B-cell ALL, is treated differently from standard B-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d8">B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="B-CELL LYMPHOBLASTIC LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma occurs when B-cell lymphoblasts grow mostly in lymph nodes instead of the bone marrow, a presentation of B-ALL. When B-cell lymphoblasts grow mostly in the lymph nodes instead of the bone marrow, B-ALL is called B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d8">B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="T-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">B-cell ALL is more common than T-cell ALL.</data>
      <data key="d8">B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="ALL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">B-cell ALL is a subtype of ALL.</data>
      <data key="d8">B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BURKITT LEUKEMIA" target="BURKITT LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Burkitt leukemia and Burkitt lymphoma are closely related conditions. Burkitt leukemia is essentially the same disease as Burkitt lymphoma, sharing the same underlying pathology. However, despite this fundamental similarity, the two are treated differently in clinical practice. The distinction reflects differences in how the disease presents and the treatment approach chosen, but the core disease process is considered the same entity.</data>
      <data key="d8">BURKITT LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BURKITT LEUKEMIA" target="ALL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Burkitt leukemia is a rare subtype of ALL.</data>
      <data key="d8">BURKITT LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
