      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Lymph node exam is recommended after diagnosis of anal squamous cell carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANAL CANAL CANCER" target="CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Anal canal cancer is a form of cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ESD" target="PELVIC MRI" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Pelvic MRI with contrast is recommended at intervals during surveillance after ESD</data>
      <data key="d8">PELVIC MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLORECTAL CANCER" target="RECTAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Rectal cancer is a colorectal cancer that forms in the rectum</data>
      <data key="d8">RECTAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROGRESSIVE DISEASE" target="ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surgery to remove the anus is recommended for locally progressive anal cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROGRESSIVE DISEASE" target="IMMUNOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Immunotherapy may be tried for locally progressive anal cancer before proceeding with surgery</data>
      <data key="d8">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROGRESSION" target="IMMUNOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">For patients whose cancer shows progression either during or after chemotherapy, immunotherapy is recommended as the next line of treatment for those who are eligible. This approach applies when the disease advances despite or following completion of chemotherapy, establishing immunotherapy as the standard subsequent therapeutic option.</data>
      <data key="d8">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEMATOPATHOLOGIST" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team may include a hematopathologist.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEMATOPATHOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEMATOPATHOLOGIST" target="BONE MARROW BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A hematopathologist analyzes cells and tissues removed during a biopsy, including a bone marrow biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEMATOPATHOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MATURE B-CELL ALL (BURKITT LEUKEMIA)" target="BURKITT LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Burkitt leukemia is essentially the same as Burkitt lymphoma, a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d8">MATURE B-CELL ALL (BURKITT LEUKEMIA)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL" target="BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">These are distinct B-ALL subtypes classified by BCR::ABL1 status and are discussed in separate chapters of the NCCN guidelines.</data>
      <data key="d8">BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CCN CANCER CENTERS" target="NCCN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CCN Cancer Centers is listed at NCCN.org/cancercenters on the NCCN website. NCCN's website at NCCN.org/cancercenters lists CCN Cancer Centers.</data>
      <data key="d8">CCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OSTOMY" target="COLOSTOMY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A colostomy is a type of ostomy that connects the colon to the abdominal surface</data>
      <data key="d8">COLOSTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BOWEL BLOCKAGE" target="COLOSTOMY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bowel blockage can be treated with a colectomy with colostomy, or a colostomy first followed by second surgery.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLOSTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BOWEL BLOCKAGE" target="COLECTOMY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A colectomy can unblock the bowel; this is called a one-stage colectomy.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYPECTOMY" target="COLECTOMY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If a polypectomy is not sufficient, surgery/resection of a larger part of the colon (colectomy) may be needed.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-CELL ALL" target="BURKITT LEUKEMIA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Burkitt leukemia is a rare subtype of B-cell ALL, also called mature B-cell ALL.</data>
      <data key="d8">BURKITT LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-CELL ALL" target="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">B-cell ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d8">B-CELL ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHRONIC MYELOID LEUKEMIA" target="BCR::ABL1" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">BCR::ABL1 produces a protein that leads to uncontrolled blood cell growth, ultimately causing CML.</data>
      <data key="d8">BCR::ABL1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHRONIC MYELOID LEUKEMIA" target="PHILADELPHIA CHROMOSOME" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Philadelphia chromosome is the hallmark of CML; if it is absent, CML is not present.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHRONIC MYELOID LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHRONIC MYELOID LEUKEMIA" target="BLAST PHASE CML" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Blast phase CML is an advanced form of CML, and the type of blast cells in BP-CML affects treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHRONIC MYELOID LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PHILADELPHIA CHROMOSOME" target="BCR::ABL1" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both are formed from the same translocation between chromosome 9 and chromosome 22: BCR::ABL1 is the fused gene, and the Philadelphia chromosome is the resulting abnormal chromosome 22.</data>
      <data key="d8">BCR::ABL1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BONE MARROW ASPIRATE" target="DIAGNOSTIC TESTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A bone marrow aspirate is a diagnostic test used to confirm AML</data>
      <data key="d8">DIAGNOSTIC TESTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LUMBAR PUNCTURE" target="DIAGNOSTIC TESTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A lumbar puncture is a diagnostic test that checks spinal fluid for leukemia cells</data>
      <data key="d8">DIAGNOSTIC TESTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OPEN BIOPSY" target="NEUROPATHOLOGIST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Tissue from an open biopsy is examined by the neuropathologist.</data>
      <data key="d8">NEUROPATHOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEREOTACTIC BIOPSY" target="NEUROPATHOLOGIST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A stereotactic biopsy sample is sent to the laboratory where the neuropathologist analyzes it.</data>
      <data key="d8">NEUROPATHOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
  </graph>
